{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b05b8de8671ad82a", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tJOURNAL OF LATEX CLASS FILES, VOL. 14, NO. 8, AUGUST 2015\n2\t\n3\t1\n4\t\n5\tTowards Real-world Environment-aware Zero-shot\n6\tText-to-speech Synthesis via Disentangled Audio\n7\tInfilling\n8\t\n9\tarXiv:2608.03011v1 [eess.AS] 4 Aug 2026\n10\t\n11\tYe-Xin Lu, Xin Wang, Member, IEEE, Yang Ai, Member, IEEE, Hui-Peng Du, Student Member, IEEE, Zhen-Hua\n12\tLing, Senior Member, IEEE, and Junichi Yamagishi, Senior Member, IEEE\n13\t\n14\tAbstract—Recent zero-shot text-to-speech (TTS) systems\n15\tachieve remarkable naturalness and speaker similarity but typically require high-quality speaker prompts and either strip\n16\taway or entangle the acoustic environment with speaker characteristics, limiting their real-world applicability. We present\n17\tan extended DAIEN-TTS, an environment-aware zero-shot TTS\n18\tframework that disentangles and jointly models speech, background noise, and reverberation, enabling independent control\n19\tover timbre and acoustic environment through separate speaker\n20\tand environment prompts. Built upon the flow-matching-based\n21\tF5-TTS, it uses a speech-environment separation module to\n22\tdecompose environmental speech into speech, noise, and reverberation components, which are injected into the Diffusion\n23\tTransformer for environment-aware generation. Training uses\n24\tsimulated data constructed by mixing clean speech with noise and\n25\troom impulse responses, together with a cross-speaker conditioning strategy that suppresses speaker information leakage from\n26\tthe environment branch. When real-world data are available,\n27\tthe system can be further fine-tuned to bridge the simulated-toreal domain gap. At inference, a triple classifier-free guidance\n28\tmechanism enables fine-grained control over speech, noise, and\n29\treverberation, and a signal-to-noise-ratio adaptation strategy\n30\taligns the synthesized speech with the environment prompt.\n31\tExperiments on simulated and real-world test sets show that\n32\tDAIEN-TTS generates environmental personalized speech with\n33\thigh naturalness, strong speaker similarity, and faithful noise and\n34\treverberation reproduction, while offering controllability beyond\n35\tprior environment-aware TTS systems.\n36\tIndex Terms—Environment-aware text-to-speech, zero-shot\n37\tTTS, disentangled audio infilling, flow matching\n38\t\n39\tI. I NTRODUCTION\n40\t\n41\tZ\n42\t\n43\tERO-SHOT text-to-speech (TTS) is used to synthesize\n44\tspeech that preserves the voice characteristics of a target\n45\tspeaker, using only a few seconds of audio prompt, without\n46\trequiring any speaker-specific training data. Current zero-shot\n47\tTTS methods can be broadly categorized into two types.\n48\tSpeaker-embedding-based methods [2]–[4] extract a fixed\n49\tspeaker representation from the audio prompt which is then\n50\tused as a conditioning input to control the timbre of the\n51\tThis work is the extended version of our conference paper [1] published at\n52\tIEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing\n53\t2026 (ICASSP 2026).\n54\tY.-X. Lu, Y. Ai, H.-P. Du and Z.-H. Ling are with the National Engineering Research Center of Speech and Language Information Processing,\n55\tUniversity of Science and Technology of China, Hefei, China (e-mail:\n56\tyxlu0102@mail.ustc.edu.cn, yangai@ustc.edu.cn, redmist@mail.ustc.edu.cn,\n57\tzhling@ustc.edu.cn). X. Wang and J. Yamagishi are with the National Institute\n58\tof Informatics, Tokyo, Japan (e-mail: wangxin@nii.ac.jp, jyamagis@nii.ac.jp).\n59\t\n60\tsynthesized speech. More recently, in-context-learning (ICL)based methods, including neural-codec-based language modeling [5], [6], flow-matching-based speech infilling [7], [8], and\n61\ttheir hybrids [9]–[11], directly leverage the audio prompt as\n62\tcontext for generation, achieving substantial improvements in\n63\tnaturalness and speaker similarity. Despite their differences,\n64\tboth types are typically trained on large-scale high-quality\n65\tspeech corpora, thus requiring high-quality audio prompts and\n66\ttending to produce clean personalized speech.\n67\tIn practice, however, audio prompts are often recorded\n68\tunder diverse real-world acoustic conditions, where various\n69\tenvironmental factors such as background noise and room reverberation are inevitably present. The two types of TTS methods exhibit different behaviors when encountering such environmental factors. Speaker-embedding-based methods encode\n70\tthe speaker characteristics of the audio prompt into a global\n71\tembedding using pretrained environment-robust speaker encoders [12], [13], thus inherently discarding frame-level environmental information and producing clean synthesized speech\n72\tregardless of the acoustic condition of the audio prompt.\n73\tICL-based methods, on the other hand, tend to preserve\n74\tthe environmental characteristics of the audio prompt in the\n75\tsynthesized speech. However, the environmental information is\n76\tinherently entangled with the speaker characteristics, making\n77\tit difficult to, for example, reproduce these characteristics from\n78\tone recording while applying the acoustic environment from\n79\tanother. When the audio prompt contains severe environmental\n80\tdistortions, the synthesized speech with these methods often\n81\texhibit degraded intelligibility and naturalness [14], [15].\n82\tIn summary, current zero-shot TTS methods either discard\n83\tor entangle the acoustic environment with speaker characteristics, without providing explicit control over the environment\n84\tof the synthesized speech. In many real-world application\n85\tscenarios, however, the acoustic environment present in the\n86\tspeech signal is not merely an undesired distortion, but rather\n87\tconstitutes a desired auditory attribute of the synthesized\n88\tspeech. For instance, in AI audiobook production, speech often\n89\tneeds to be rendered with background sounds that match the\n90\tnarrative scene, such as a bustling street or quiet room [16],\n91\t[17]. In the generation of synthetic training data for automatic\n92\tspeech recognition (ASR) [18], [19] or automatic speaker\n93\tverification (ASV), realistic reproduction of diverse acoustic\n94\tenvironments is essential to ensure that the trained models generalize well to real-world conditions. These applications call\n95\tfor an environment-aware TTS system that can independently\n96\t\n97\t\fJOURNAL OF LATEX CLASS FILES, VOL. 14, NO. 8, AUGUST 2015\n98\t\n99\tspecify the speaker identity and acoustic environment of the\n100\tsynthesized speech.\n101\tSeveral studies have explored environment-aware TTS to\n102\tindependently control the timbre and acoustic environment\n103\tof the synthesized speech through separate speaker and environment audio prompts. Current TTS systems [20], [21]\n104\tuse dedicated speaker and environment encoders to extract\n105\tglobal embeddings for disentangled control but are limited\n106\tto time-invariant acoustic environments, such as reverberation, and cannot handle time-varying background noise. In\n107\taddition, controllable masked speech prediction [22] has been\n108\tintroduced to selectively remove or preserve the background\n109\tsound of the speaker prompt; however, the acoustic environment of the synthesized speech is limited to a binary choice\n110\tbetween preservation and removal, and cannot be specified\n111\tindependently of the speaker prompt. From the text-to-audio\n112\t(TTA) generation perspective, systems such as VoiceLDM [16]\n113\tand VoiceDiT [17] have the ability to generate environmental\n114\taudio containing speech but are not designed for TTS and lack\n115\tzero-shot speaker cloning capability or exhibit poor alignment\n116\tbetween speech and environment. In our prior work [1], we\n117\tproposed the TTS system DAIEN-TTS, which introduces a\n118\tspeech-environment separation (SES) module to disentangle\n119\tthe background noise from the speech prompt and achieves\n120\ttime-varying ENvironment-aware TTS via Disentangled Audio Infilling. However, we only considered background noise\n121\twithout modeling reverberation and trained exclusively on\n122\tsimulated data, leaving a domain gap when applied to realworld recordings.\n123\tFor this work, we extended DAIEN-TTS to address these\n124\tlimitations towards real-world environment-aware zero-shot\n125\tTTS. Specifically, we extended the SES module to jointly\n126\tmodel background noise and reverberation, where the noise\n127\tis represented as a frame-level mel-spectrogram and the reverberation is encoded as an utterance-level embedding. We\n128\tfurther introduce a cross-speaker conditioning strategy during\n129\ttraining to prevent speaker information leakage from the environment branch. At inference, we extend the dual classifierfree guidance (DCFG) to a triple CFC (TCFG) mechanism,\n130\tenabling independent control over the speech, noise, and reverberation components, along with a signal-to-noise ratio (SNR)\n131\tadaptation strategy to align the synthesized speech with the\n132\tenvironment prompt. Finally, to bridge the simulated-to-real\n133\tdomain gap, the system is first trained on simulated data then\n134\tfine-tuned on real-world speech data. Extensive experiments\n135\ton both simulated and real-world test sets demonstrate that the\n136\textended DAIEN-TTS generates environmental personalized\n137\tspeech with high naturalness, strong speaker similarity, faithful\n138\tenvironment reconstruction, and generalizes well to real-world\n139\tacoustic conditions.\n140\tThe main contributions of this paper are summarized as\n141\tfollows:\n142\t1) We present an extended DAIEN-TTS framework that\n143\tjointly models speech, background noise, and reverberation, enabling independent control over timbre and acoustic environment through separate audio prompts.\n144\t2) We present our designed two-stage SES module and\n145\tcross-attention-based environment-conditioning mecha-\n146\t\n147\t2\n148\t\n149\tText Prompt\n150\tz\n151\t<latexit sha1_base64=\"V8RfMA1dva6qJX4yKlb8sNAEq4Y=\"></latexit>\n152\t\n153\tSpk. Prompt\n154\tyspk\n155\t<latexit sha1_base64=\"9uid+6ovUmDy5LyOMgZkfeFHFKc=\"></latexit>\n156\t\n157\tEnv. Prompt\n158\tyenv\n159\t<latexit sha1_base64=\"l0zLhsE0+OgJ8sqwQ5XwM1ANtL0=\"></latexit>\n160\t\n161\tSpeaker\n162\tCharacteristics\n163\tAcoustic\n164\tEvironment\n165\t\n166\tEnvironmentAware TTS\n167\t\n168\tEnvironmental\n169\tPersonalized Speech\n170\t\n171\tFig. 1. Task formulation of environment-aware zero-shot TTS with separate\n172\ttext, speaker, and environment prompts\n173\t\n174\tnism to disentangle and reintegrate noise and reverberation into the generation process.\n175\t3) We introduce a TCFG mechanism and SNR adaptation\n176\tstrategy for fine-grained and independent control over the\n177\tspeech, noise, and reverberation components at inference.\n178\t4) We demonstrate that the extended DAIEN-TTS system\n179\ttrained on simulated data can be further fine-tuned on\n180\treal-world speech data when available, effectively bridging the simulated-to-real domain gap.\n181\t5) We conducted extensive evaluations on both simulated\n182\tand real-world test sets, demonstrating the effectiveness\n183\tof the extended DAIEN-TTS framework.\n184\tII. P ROBLEM F ORMULATION\n185\tIn real-world speech recordings, the captured signal is not\n186\tonly determined by the speech content and speaker’s voice\n187\tbut also shaped by the acoustic environment. The acoustic\n188\tenvironment encompasses a variety of factors that can be\n189\tbroadly categorized into two types: time-varying factors, such\n190\tas background noise and ambient sound effects, and timeinvariant factors, such as room reverberation and channel\n191\tdistortions. Considering a clean speech signal s, additive\n192\ttime-varying environmental signal n, and room impulse response (RIR) h representing the time-invariant component, the\n193\trecorded environmental speech can be approximated as\n194\ty = s ∗ h + n,\n195\t\n196\t(1)\n197\t\n198\twhere ∗ denotes convolution. Throughout this paper, we use\n199\tthe term environmental speech to refer to speech recorded\n200\tunder such non-clean acoustic conditions, where various environmental factors may be present.\n201\tSpeech signals therefore carry at least three types of information: semantic content, speaker characteristics, and acoustic\n202\tenvironment. The goal with environment-aware TTS is to\n203\tdisentangle these factors and recombine them in a controllable\n204\tmanner such that each can be independently specified through\n205\tseparate inputs. As illustrated in Fig. 1, given a text input z,\n206\tspeaker audio prompt yspk , and environment audio prompt\n207\tyenv , the system synthesizes a new environmental speech that\n208\tconveys the semantic content specified by z, preserves the\n209\tspeaker characteristics extracted from yspk , and reproduces\n210\tthe acoustic environment extracted from yenv . Both yspk and\n211\tyenv are environmental speech recordings, as expressed with\n212\tEq. (1), from which the speaker information (i.e., encoded in\n213\ts) and environment information (i.e., encoded in n and h)\n214\tare respectively disentangled and recombined for generation.\n215\tNote that we do not know ground-truth n and h for generation.\n216\tWhile the text input and speaker prompt provide direct control\n217\t\n218\t\fJOURNAL OF LATEX CLASS FILES, VOL. 14, NO. 8, AUGUST 2015\n219\t\n220\t3\n221\t\n222\t<latexit sha1_base64=\"FodIBRguWiBaaRvn1J7PD4usg/w=\"></latexit>\n223\t\n224\t<latexit sha1_base64=\"FodIBRguWiBaaRvn1J7PD4usg/w=\"></latexit>\n225\t\n226\tmspk → (x1 ↑ x0 )\n227\t\n228\tmspk → (x1 ↑ x0 )\n229\t\n230\tMasked\n231\t\n232\tF ozen\n233\t\n234\tMasked\n235\t\n236\tT a nab e\n237\t\n