n roduces\n534\two core componen s o enab e env ronmen -aware genera on\n535\tan SES modu e (F g 3) for d sen ang ng speaker and env ronmen nforma on and env ronmen cond on ng mechan sm n\n536\the TTS modu e (F g 2) for con ro ab e recomb na on\n537\tThe SES modu e (Sec III-B) decomposes an env ronmen a\n538\tspeech s gna n o a c ean speech componen no se componen and reverbera on componen wh ch are conver ed\n539\tn o cond ons for he TTS modu e me -spec rograms cspk\n540\tand cno se and an u erance- eve reverbera on embedd ng\n541\t\n542\tSES Modu e\n543\tY\n544\t\n545\tM k ngN\n546\t\n547\tM\n548\t\n549\ty\n550\t\n551\tN\n552\t\n553\tW\n554\t\n555\tY\n556\t\n557\tM k ngN\n558\t\n559\t→\n560\t\n561\tR\n562\t\n563\tW\n564\t\n565\t→\n566\t\n567\tW\n568\t\n569\tY\n570\t\n571\t→\n572\t\n573\tY\n574\t\n575\tF g 3 Ove v ew o he SES modu e w h wo s age no se and eve be a on\n576\tsepa a on\n577\t\n578\tcreverb The TTS modu e (Sec III-C) genera es he arge\n579\tenv ronmen a me -spec rogram cond oned on hese fac ors\n580\ta ong w h he ex npu wh ch s subsequen y conver ed o\n581\ta waveform by us ng a vocoder\n582\tDur ng ra n ng (Sec III-D) he wo modu es are ra ned\n583\tsepara e y us ng s mu a ed da a w h ground- ru h s gna s\n584\tw h a cross-speaker cond on ng s ra egy o preven speaker\n585\tnforma on eakage When rea -wor d da a are ava ab e he\n586\tex ended DAIEN-TTS can be fur her fine- uned (Sec III-E)\n587\tby connec ng he wo modu es n o a fu d sen ang emen recomb na on p pe ne A nference (Sec III-F) TCFG and\n588\tSNR adap a on enab e ndependen con ro over he speech\n589\tno se and reverbera on componen s\n590\tB SES Modu e\n591\tT me-frequency mask ng s a we -es ab shed echn que\n592\tfor aud o source separa on and speech enhancemen [23]\n593\t[24] where a mask s es ma ed and app ed o he m x ure\n594\tspec rogram o ex rac or suppress arge s gna componen s I\n595\thas a so been shown o be effec ve for suppress ng convo u ve\n596\t\n597\t\fJOURNAL OF LATEX CLASS FILES, VOL. 14, NO. 8, AUGUST 2015\n598\t\n599\t4\n600\t\n601\tdistortions such as reverberation [25]. Since the downstream\n602\tTTS module operates on mel-spectrograms derived from magnitude spectrograms and does not require phase information,\n603\tour SES module is designed to execute masking in the\n604\tmagnitude spectrogram domain. As illustrated in Fig. 3, the\n605\tSES module adopts a cascaded two-stage design, where the\n606\tfirst stage separates noise and the second stage separates\n607\treverberation.\n608\tIn the first stage, the input environmental speech y is\n609\tsplit into two components: a reverberant speech component and noise component. This is achieved by transforming y into a magnitude spectrogram |Y| ∈ RF ×L via the\n610\tshort-time Fourier transform, where F and L denote the\n611\tnumber of frequency bins and time frames, respectively. A\n612\tTransformer-based [26] masking network for noise separation\n613\t(MaskingNet-N) then predicts two complementary separation\n614\tmasks WS+R and WN ∈ RF ×L , which are applied to |Y|\n615\tvia element-wise multiplication:\n616\t|YS+R | = |Y| ⊙ WN ,\n617\t\n618\t|YN | = |Y| ⊙ WS+R .\n619\t\n620\t(2)\n621\t\n622\tIn the second stage, the reverberation is further separated from the reverberant speech. Another masking network\n623\t(MaskingNet-R) predicts a reverberation mask WR ∈ RF ×L ,\n624\tand the clean speech magnitude spectrogram is obtained by\n625\t|YS | = |YS+R | ⊙ WR .\n626\t\n627\t(3)\n628\t\n629\tThe SES module thus outputs three disentangled components: |YS |, |YN |, and WR . Note that unlike noise, which\n630\tis additive and can be directly separated from the mixture as\n631\tan independent signal, reverberation is a convolutive effect\n632\tinseparable from the speech content in the spectro-temporal\n633\tdomain. Therefore, rather than attempting to recover the RIR,\n634\twe use the reverberation mask WR as an implicit representation of the reverberation characteristics, as it encodes the\n635\tspectro-temporal distortion introduced by the room acoustics.\n636\tHow these three components are transformed into conditions\n637\tfor the TTS module is described in the next subsection.\n638\tC. Environment-aware TTS Module\n639\tOur TTS module is built upon F5-TTS [8], which formulates zero-shot TTS as a text-guided speech infilling task [7]\n640\tunder the conditional flow matching (CFM) framework [27].\n641\tGiven a speech mel-spectrogram x1 and its corresponding text\n642\tsequence z, a random span mask m is applied to x1 , where the\n643\tunmasked portion serves as the speaker prompt. Starting from\n644\ta sampled Gaussian noise x0 ∼ N (0, I), the noisy input is\n645\tconstructed as ψt (x0 ) = (1−t)x0 +tx1 , where t is a randomly\n646\tsampled flow step. A Diffusion Transformer (DiT) [28] based\n647\tnetwork Vt is trained to predict the velocity field that transforms x0 towards x1 , conditioned on the unmasked portion\n648\t(1−m)⊙x1 and the full text sequence z, which includes both\n649\tthe transcription of the unmasked prompt and the text to be\n650\tgenerated. To extend this framework for environment-aware\n651\tgeneration, we reformulate the task as a disentangled audio\n652\tinfilling problem, where the model simultaneously infills the\n653\tmasked speech and its corresponding acoustic environment,\n654\t\n655\tconditioned on the text and the disentangled speaker and\n656\tenvironment representations.\n657\tThe environment-aware TTS module takes three conditions\n658\tas input: a frame-level speaker condition cspk , frame-level\n659\tnoise condition cnoise , and utterance-level reverberation embedding creverb . In the pretraining stage, the training target\n660\t(y at the bottom of Fig. 2 (a)) is a simulated environmental\n661\tspeech from a clean speech signal s, noise signal n, and RIR\n662\th, as expressed with Eq. (1). The cspk and cnoise are obtained\n663\tby directly converting s and n into mel-spectrograms. The\n664\tcreverb is extracted by convolving a different speaker’s clean\n665\tspeech s′ with the same RIR h, computing the reverberation\n666\tmask WR from the resulting clean-reverberant pair, and\n667\tpassing it through an ECAPA-TDNN [13] encoder. This crossspeaker conditioning strategy ensures that creverb captures\n668\tonly the room acoustic characteristics without leaking the\n669\ttarget speaker’s identity. In the fine-tuning stage (Fig. 2 (b)),\n670\tthe training target is real-world environmental speech, and the\n671\tthree conditions are provided by the SES module applied to\n672\tthe same input.\n673\tDuring training, random span masks of varying lengths\n674\tmspeech and mnoise are independently applied to the disentangled clean speech mel-spectrogram cspk and noise melspectrogram cnoise , respectively. The reverberation embedding\n675\tcreverb is an utterance-level representation, thus does not require masking. During inference, the speaker and environment\n676\tconditions are derived from two separate audio recordings\n677\tthat are generally of different lengths. The clean speech\n678\tcondition is temporally aligned with the text sequence, the\n679\tnoise condition has no such constraint, and the reverberation\n680\tembedding is utterance-level, thus independent of the prompt\n681\tlength. Applying masks of independent lengths during training\n682\tenables the model to accommodate this length mismatch at\n683\tinference, avoiding truncation or padding of the environment\n684\tcondition that could lead to loss of environment information.\n685\tThe three conditions are injected into the DiT blocks\n686\tthrough different mechanisms, as depicted in the middle of\n687\tFig. 2. The unmasked speaker condition (1 − mspeech ) ⊙ cspk\n688\tis concatenated with the text embedding z and the noisy input\n689\tψt (x0 ) along the feature dimension, following the original F5TTS design. For the environment conditions, the reverberation\n690\tembedding creverb is first repeated along the frame dimension\n691\tto match the length of the noise condition then added to the\n692\tunmasked noise condition (1−mnoise )⊙cnoise to form a unified environment representation. This combined environment\n693\tcondition is introduced via a multi-head cross-attention layer\n694\tinserted into each DiT block, enabling the model to jointly\n695\tattend to frame-level noise patterns and global reverberation\n696\tcharacteristics. With these conditions, the model learns to\n697\treconstruct the masked environmental speech mel-spectrogram\n698\tmspeech ⊙ x1 , including both the personalized speech content\n699\tand its corresponding acoustic environment.\n700\tD. Pretraining on Simulated Data\n701\tAs illustrated in Fig. 2 (a), we construct simulated environmental speech by mixing clean speech with noise and RIRs at\n702\tvarious SNR levels, providing paired data with known groundtruth components for supervised training of both modules.\n703\t\n704\t\fJOURNAL OF LATEX CLASS FILES, VOL. 14, NO. 8, AUGUST 2015\n705\t\n706\t5\n707\t\n708\tEnvironmental\n709\tPersonalized Speech\n710\t\n711\tThe SES module is pretrained independently with a combination of magnitude spectrogram and mel-spectrogram reconstruction losses over all three separated components:\n712\tLSES = Lmag + Lmel ,\n713\t\n714\tTarget Mel\n715\t\n716\tDiscard\n717\t\n718\tODE Solver\n719\t\n720\t(4)\n721\tvt\n722\t\n723\tSway Sampled\n724\tTime Step t\n725\t\n726\twhere\n727\t\n728\t+ |ŶN | − |YN |\n729\t\n730\t2\n731\t2\n732\t\n733\t2\n734\t\n735\t<latexit sha1_base64=\"/gXV1hjf8BVEJzoOhCDiRduwqD8=\"></latexit>\n736\t\n737\tN → DiT Blocks\n738\t\n739\t+ |ŶS | − |YS |\n740\t\n741\t2\n742\t2\n743\t\n744\t<latexit sha1_base64=\"7lfWxdcM0w6+1+eZt9k6zXGaw+c=\"></latexit>\n745\t\n746\t(5)\n747\t\n748\tC\n749\t\n750\tx0 → N (I, 0)\n751\t<latexit sha1_base64=\"8gaUj6IWXvmma+m+gaBmI4bPEX4=\"></latexit>\n752\t\n753\t,\n754\tzref\n755\tText\n756\t\n757\tzref ·gen T H E\n758\t\n759\t1\n760\t\n761\tGaussian Noise\n762\t\n763\tzgen\n764\t\n765\t<latexit sha1_base64=\"qEuDxMgdEAJgqOToVwRjbWL1g4w=\"></latexit>\n766\t\n767\t<latexit sha1_base64=\"15iOQdwaPD172saHm86DzPRdkHo=\"></latexit>\n768\t\n769\tP R I C E\n770\t\n771\t<latexit sha1_base64=\"UN80LFVIE5ruU8TafbjeaLPpNB4=\"></latexit>\n772\t\n773\tLmel = Mel(|ŶS+R |) − Mel(|YS+R |)\n774\t\n775\t<latexit sha1_base64=\"qsk3mpAkyyHYS5S47vRny3Ok9Hc=\"></latexit>\n776\t\n777\t<latexit sha1_base64=\"pC5O0yYlY6G1Vk4VdWIJxXDJLx0=\"></latexit>\n778\t\n779\tConvNeXt V2\n780\t\n781\t2\n782\t\n783\tECAPATDNN\n784\t\n785\tcreverb\n786\tcnoise\n787\t\n788\t<latexit sha1_base64=\"sk80zL9UtHmK7/DxTVagmPDPbv4=\"></latexit>\n789\t\n790\t<latexit sha1_base64=\"wIuqdg4I+aI5/95yudiXsxGS9Ag=\"></latexit>\n791\t\n792\tLmag = |ŶS+R | − |YS+R |\n793\t\n794\tVocoder\n795\t\n796\tcspk\n797\t\n798\tH E … H I G H . <F> <F> <F>\n799\t\n800\tTo Generate\n801\t\n802\t<latexit sha1_base64=\"0ceIEsSVpBXYQMRm4fCYfQ23Fe4=\"></latexit>\n803\t\n804\t+ Mel(|ŶS |) − Mel(|YS |) ,\n805\tcnoise\n806\tTo Generate\n807\t1\n808\t1\n809\t(6)\n810\tN\n811\tN\n812\t|Yspk\n813\t|\n814\t|Yenv\n815\t|\n816\t|Ŷ| and |Y| denote the predicted and corresponding groundR\n817\tR\n818\tS\n819\tS\n820\tWspk\n821\tWenv\n822\tSES Module\n823\t|Yspk\n824\t|\n825\t|Yenv\n826\t|\n827\tSES Module\n828\ttruth magnitude spectrogram, and Mel(·) denotes the log-melspectrogram conversion via a mel filter bank.\n829\tyspk\n830\tyenv\n831\tSpk. Prompt\n832\tEnv. Prompt\n833\tThe TTS module is pretrained separately using the conditional flow matching objective:\n834\tFig. 4. Inference process of the extended DAIEN-TTS. Given text z, a speaker\n835\t\u0002\n836\tyspk , and environment prompt yenv , the SES module extracts the\n837\tLCFM = Vt ψt (x0 )|z, (1 − mspeech ) ⊙ cspk , (1 − mnoise ) prompt\n838\tspeaker and environment conditions from the speaker prompt and environment\n839\tprompt, respectively, for generation.\n840\t2\n841\t\u0001\n842\t\u0003\n843\t⊙ cnoise , creverb − (x1 − x0 ) ⊙ mspeech ,\n844\t+ Mel(|ŶN |) − Mel(|YN |)\n845\t\n846\t<latexit sha1_base64=\"qsk3mpAkyyHYS5S47vRny3Ok9Hc=\"></latexit>\n847\t\n848\tMel\n849\t\n850\t<latexit sha1_base64=\"Xos59SmyrSNElNhb0Ezl91IGQmc=\"></latexit>\n851\t\n852\t<latexit sha1_base64=\"zaPgjQiWPrJGYOwnNkEAHuU0fb0=\"></latexit>\n853\t\n854\tMel\n855\t\n856\t<latexit sha1_base64=\"c5QZum/rX4nM0B5E2MMU5748Pqc=\"></latexit>\n857\t\n858\t<latexit sha1_base64=\"7YIXNO6512D+nK/RPSDQPT6w8Vg=\"></latexit>\n859\t\n860\t<latexit sha1_base64=\"pTdOpy+3in/Pgw3ORp1gwIIUcXk=\"></latexit>\n861\t\n862\t<latexit sha1_base64=\"+06IGHTryUCydNvRyB30dPoiiJE=\"></latexit>\n863\t\n864\t<latexit sha1_base64=\"ZQ+nMskbG8V6+7ik+9luU1eOItc=\"></latexit>\n865\t\n866\t<latexit sha1_base64=\"AcQ6Sc1tk8nnuQDe9qUkOWP73dY=\"></latexit>\n867\t\n868\t2\n869\t\n870\t(7)\n871\t\n872\twhere Vt (·) is the learned velocity field. As described in\n873\tSec. III-C, the reverberation mask used for extracting creverb\n874\tis computed from a different speaker’s utterance to prevent\n875\tspeaker information leakage. Specifically, for each training\n876\tsample, we randomly select an utterance from a different\n877\tspeaker, truncate or repeat-pad it to match the target length,\n878\tand convolve it with the same RIR applied to the original\n879\tutterance. This ensures that the reverberation mask retains\n880\tonly the room acoustic characteristics while discarding the\n881\ttarget speaker’s identity. Noise and reverberation augmentations are independently applied to each clean speech sample\n882\twith probabilities pnoise and prir , respectively, resulting in\n883\tfour possible training conditions: clean speech, noisy speech,\n884\treverberant speech, and noisy-reverberant speech. This ensures\n885\tthat the model learns to handle diverse environmental conditions while maintaining robust text-speech alignment from the\n886\tclean speech cases.\n887\tE. Fine-tuning on Real-world Data\n888\tAs illustrated in Fig. 2 (b), to bridge the domain gap\n889\tbetween simulated training data and real-world recordings,\n890\twe fine-tune the extended DAIEN-TTS on real-world environmental speech data by connecting the SES and TTS modules\n891\tinto a full pipeline. Unlike pretraining where the TTS module\n892\treceives ground-truth conditions, during fine-tuning the three\n893\tconditions cspk , cnoise , and creverb are directly provided\n894\tby the SES module applied to the real-world environmental\n895\tspeech. The TTS module is then trained to reconstruct the\n896\tsame input environmental speech using LCFM .\n897\tSeveral components are frozen during fine-tuning. The SES\n898\tmodule is frozen as real-world environmental speech lacks\n899\t\n900\tpaired clean speech references, making supervised optimization of LSES infeasible. The text encoder is frozen to preserve\n901\tthe text-speech alignment acquired during pretraining. The\n902\tECAPA-TDNN reverb encoder is frozen because ground-truth\n903\tRIRs are unavailable for real-world recordings, precluding\n904\tthe application of the cross-speaker conditioning strategy;\n905\tupdating the reverb encoder without this constraint would\n906\trisk speaker information leakage through the environment\n907\tbranch. The remaining parameters, including the DiT blocks\n908\tand environment cross-attention layers, are updated to adapt\n909\tto the acoustic characteristics of real-world environments.\n910\tF. Inference\n911\tThe inference process is illustrated in Fig. 4. Given a\n912\tspeaker audio prompt yspk and environment audio prompt\n913\tyenv , the SES module is applied to each prompt separately:\n914\tS\n915\tfrom yspk , we extract the clean speech component |Yspk\n916\t| and\n917\tconvert it to the speaker condition cspk ; from yenv , we extract\n918\tN\n919\tR\n920\tthe noise component |Yenv\n921\t| and reverberation mask Wenv\n922\t,\n923\twhich are converted to cnoise and creverb , respectively. With\n924\tthe transcription of the speaker prompt zref and the text to\n925\tbe generated zgen , we concatenate them as zref ·gen and use\n926\tan ordinary differential equation (ODE) solver to generate the\n927\ttarget mel-spectrogram. Starting from sampled Gaussian noise\n928\tx0 ∼ N (0, I), the ODE solver integrates towards the target\n929\tx1 according to\n930\t\u0001\n931\tdψt (x0 )/dt = Vt ψt (x0 ), zref ·gen , cspk , cnoise , creverb .\n932\t(8)\n933\tAfter generation, the prompt region corresponding to zref is\n934\tdiscarded, and the remaining generated mel-spectrogram is\n935\tconverted to a waveform by using the vocoder.\n936\t\n937\t\fJOURNAL OF LATEX CLASS FILES, VOL. 14, NO. 8, AUGUST 2015\n938\t\n939\t6\n940\t\n941\tThe above inference procedure generates environmental\n942\tspeech with the specified speaker and environment conditions\n943\tbut offers limited control over the contribution of each environmental component to the synthesized output. To enhance\n944\tthe controllability at inference, we further introduce two\n945\tcomplementary strategies: TCFG for independently adjusting\n946\tthe strength of the speech, noise, and reverberation components, and SNR adaptation for aligning the noise level of the\n947\tsynthesized speech with that of the environment prompt.\n948\t1) Triple Classifier-free Guidance: F5-TTS uses CFG [29]\n949\tto balance generation fidelity and diversity by treating the text\n950\tand speaker conditions as a unified guidance term. In our prior\n951\twork [1], we adopted a DCFG [16] mechanism to separately\n952\tguide the speech and environment components. In this work,\n953\tsince the environment condition is further decomposed into\n954\tnoise and reverberation, we extended DCFG to TCFG for\n955\tindependent control over the speech, noise, and reverberation\n956\tcomponents. Let cspeech = {z, cspk } denote the unified speech\n957\tcondition combining the text sequence and speaker condition.\n958\tThe TCFG is formulated as\n959\tVt,TCFG = Vt (ψt (x0 ), cspeech , cnoise , creverb )\n960\t\n961\t+ αspeech [Vt (ψt (x0 ), cspeech , ∅, ∅) − Vt (ψt (x0 ), ∅, ∅, ∅)]\n962\t+ αnoise [Vt (ψt (x0 ), ∅, cnoise , ∅) − Vt (ψt (x0 ), ∅, ∅, ∅)]\n963\t\n964\t+ αreverb [Vt (ψt (x0 ), ∅, ∅, creverb ) − Vt (ψt (x0 ), ∅, ∅, ∅)] ,\n965\t(9)\n966\twhere ∅ denotes the null condition, and αspeech , αnoise , and\n967\tαreverb are the guidance scales for the speech, noise, and\n968\treverberation components, respectively. The derivation follows\n969\tnaturally from the DCFG formulation [16] by extending to\n970\tthree independent conditions. Adjusting these three scales independently enables fine-grained control over the contribution\n971\tof each component to the synthesized environmental speech.\n972\t2) SNR Adaptation: The SNR of the environment prompt\n973\tcharacterizes the relative prominence of the background noise\n974\tin the acoustic scene, thus an important attribute to be preserved in the synthesized speech. However, since the speaker\n975\tand environment conditions are extracted from separate audio\n976\tprompts with potentially different energy levels, a direct combination of these conditions may result in an SNR discrepancy\n977\tbetween the synthesized speech and environment prompt.\n978\tTo mitigate this, we introduce an SNR adaptation strategy\n979\tthat rescales the noise condition such that the SNR of the\n980\tsynthesized speech is consistent with that of the environment\n981\tprompt.\n982\tConsidering the environmental speech y is composed of\n983\tits reverberant speech component yS+R and noise component\n984\tyN , such that y = yS+R + yN , the SNR can be expressed as\n985\tSNR = 10 log10\n986\t\n987\t∥YS+R ∥22\n988\t∥yS+R ∥22\n989\t=\n990\t10\n991\tlog\n992\t,\n993\t10\n994\t∥yN ∥22\n995\t∥YN ∥22\n996\t\n997\t(10)\n998\t\n999\twhere the second equality follows from Parseval’s theorem.\n1000\tTo match the SNR of the synthesized speech with that of the\n1001\tenvironment prompt, we require\n1002\tS+R 2\n1003\t∥Yspk\n1004\t∥2\n1005\t\n1006\tS+R 2\n1007\t∥Yenv\n1008\t∥2\n1009\t10 log10\n1010\t= 10 log10\n1011\t,\n1012\t2\n1013\tN\n1014\tN\n1015\t∥Yenv · Scale∥2\n1016\t∥Yenv ∥22\n1017\t\n1018\tS+R\n1019\twhere Yspk\n1020\tdenotes the separated reverberant speech magniS+R\n1021\tN\n1022\ttude spectrogram of the speaker prompt, and Yenv\n1023\tand Yenv\n1024\tdenote the separated reverberant speech and noise magnitude\n1025\tspectrograms of the environment prompt, respectively. Solving\n1026\tfor the scaling factor yields\n1027\ts\n1028\tS+R 2\n1029\t∥Yspk\n1030\t∥2\n1031\t.\n1032\t(12)\n1033\tScale =\n1034\tS+R 2\n1035\t∥Yenv ∥2\n1036\tN\n1037\tDuring inference, |Yenv\n1038\t| is multiplied by this factor prior to\n1039\tmel-spectrogram conversion, ensuring that the resulting noise\n1040\tcondition reflects the target SNR of the environment prompt\n1041\trelative to the speech energy of the speaker prompt.\n1042\t\n1043\tIV. E XPERIMENTAL S ETUP\n1044\tA. Datasets\n1045\tOur experiments involve two types of data: simulated\n1046\tenvironmental speech for pretraining and evaluation under\n1047\tcontrolled conditions, and real-world environmental speech for\n1048\tfine-tuning and evaluation under realistic conditions.\n1049\t1) Simulated Data: For pretraining, we used the LibriTTS\n1050\tcorpus [30], which contains 580 hours of high-quality English\n1051\tspeech data. To construct simulated environmental speech, the\n1052\tclean speech was mixed with noise samples from the DNSChallenge noise set [31], which comprises approximately 180\n1053\thours of environmental audio sourced from AudioSet [32] and\n1054\tFreeSound [33]. RIRs were sampled from the DNS-Challenge\n1055\tRIR set 1 , containing 20,248 samples. The SNR for noise\n1056\tmixing was uniformly sampled between −5 and 15 dB, and\n1057\tthe noise and RIR augmentations were independently applied\n1058\twith probabilities pnoise and prir , both set to 0.5.\n1059\tFor evaluation under simulated conditions, we constructed\n1060\ta test set based on the Seed-TTS test-en set2 , which originally\n1061\tprovides the text to be synthesized, a speaker reference audio\n1062\twith its transcription, and a corresponding human-recorded\n1063\tground truth for each sample. To evaluate environment-aware\n1064\tTTS, we extended each sample with an environment reference\n1065\taudio by randomly selecting an utterance from a different\n1066\tspeaker. Both the speaker and environment reference audios\n1067\twere augmented with independently sampled noise and RIRs,\n1068\twith SNRs uniformly distributed between 0 and 20 dB. The\n1069\tnoise clips were sourced from SoundBible3 and the RIRs\n1070\tfrom the SRIRACHA dataset [34]. To construct the ground\n1071\ttruth for the environmental personalized speech, the original\n1072\tclean ground-truth recording was first convolved with the\n1073\tsame RIR applied to the environment reference audio. For\n1074\tthe noise component, since the TTS model is expected to\n1075\tcontinue the background noise from the environment prompt,\n1076\tthe noise signal appended to the ground truth was taken from\n1077\tthe segment immediately following the portion used in the\n1078\tenvironment reference audio, preserving temporal continuity.\n1079\tAll data were constructed at a sampling rate of 24 kHz.\n1080\t1 https://www.openslr.org/26/, https://www.openslr.org/28/\n1081\t\n1082\t(11)\n1083\t\n1084\t2 https://github.com/BytedanceSpeech/seed-tts-eval\n1085\t3 https://soundbible.com\n1086\t\n1087\t\fJOURNAL OF LATEX CLASS FILES, VOL. 14, NO. 8, AUGUST 2015\n1088\t\n1089\t2) Real-world Data: For fine-tuning, we used the TITWHard subset of the TITW (TTS in the Wild) dataset [35],\n1090\twhich is derived from VoxCeleb 1 [36] through an automated\n1091\ttranscription, segmentation, and selection pipeline, containing\n1092\t273,493 utterances totaling 184.57 hours of English speech\n1093\trecorded under diverse real-world acoustic conditions. Since\n1094\tthe original TITW data are recorded at 16 kHz, all audio\n1095\twas upsampled to 24 kHz to match the sampling rate of the\n1096\tpretraining data.\n1097\tFor evaluation under real-world conditions, we used text\n1098\tfrom the LibriTTS test-clean set, while the speaker and environment reference audios were selected from the TITW-Hard\n1099\ttest set. To ensure distinct acoustic conditions between the\n1100\ttwo references, we scored all TITW test samples using DNSMOS [37] and selected speaker prompts from samples with\n1101\tbackground-noise quality (BAK) scores above 3.5, indicating\n1102\trelatively clean recordings, and environment prompts from\n1103\tsamples with BAK scores below 2.5, indicating prominent\n1104\tbackground noise or reverberation.\n1105\t\n1106\t7\n1107\t\n1108\tit to reconstruct environmental speech waveforms from melspectrograms. 4\n1109\tC. Baseline and Compared Systems\n1110\tWe evaluated the following systems in our experiments:\n1111\t• F5-TTS [8]: The base F5-TTS model trained on the\n1112\tLibriTTS dataset for 600k steps, representing a standard\n1113\tzero-shot TTS system without environment awareness.\n1114\t• DAIEN-TTS-N [1]: The conference version of DAIENTTS, which only models background noise without reverberation and trained exclusively on simulated data.\n1115\t• DAIEN-TTS-NR: The extended DAIEN-TTS that jointly\n1116\tmodels background noise and reverberation, pretrained on\n1117\tsimulated data.\n1118\t• DAIEN-TTS-FT: The extended DAIEN-TTS fine-tuned\n1119\ton real-world speech data from the TITW-Hard dataset.\n1120\tFor a fair comparison, all systems share the same LibriTTS\n1121\tpretraining data and Vocos vocoder.\n1122\tD. Evaluation Metrics\n1123\t\n1124\tB. Model Configuration\n1125\tIn the SES module, both MaskingNet-N and MaskingNet-R\n1126\tconsist of an input convolutional layer, 8 Transformer blocks\n1127\twith 16 attention heads, an embedding dimension of 1024,\n1128\tand feed-forward network (FFN) dimension of 2048, followed\n1129\tby output convolutional layers. For the TTS module, we\n1130\tadopted the same DiT architecture as F5-TTS, with each DiT\n1131\tblock inserted by a cross-attention layer configured with 16\n1132\tattention heads for environment conditioning. The ECAPATDNN [13] encoder for reverberation embedding extraction\n1133\tis jointly trained with the TTS module during pretraining,\n1134\tand frozen during fine-tuning to prevent speaker information\n1135\tleakage. All audio was represented as 100-dimensional log\n1136\tmel-filterbank features at a sampling rate of 24 kHz with a\n1137\thop length of 256.\n1138\tBoth SES and TTS modules were pretrained for 600k steps\n1139\ton 4 NVIDIA A100 80G GPUs, using the AdamW optimizer\n1140\twith a peak learning rate of 7.5 × 10−5 , linearly warmed up\n1141\tfor 20k steps and linearly decayed thereafter, and a batch size\n1142\tof 120,000 audio frames. During training, a random 70 to\n1143\t100% of mel frames are independently masked for both the\n1144\tspeech and noise conditions, while the reverberation condition\n1145\tis always provided in full. For CFG training, the speech, noise,\n1146\tand reverberation conditions are each independently dropped\n1147\twith a probability of 0.3, and the speech condition together\n1148\twith the text input is jointly dropped with an additional\n1149\tprobability of 0.2. For fine-tuning on real-world data, the\n1150\tlearning rate was reduced to 1 × 10−5 with a warm-up of\n1151\t2k steps, and the system was fine-tuned for 50k steps. During\n1152\tinference, the TCFG guidance scales were set to αspeech = 1,\n1153\tαnoise = 3, and αreverb = 6 unless otherwise specified, and\n1154\tthe number of function evaluations for the ODE solver was\n1155\tset to 32. For the vocoder, we trained a Vocos [38] model on\n1156\tenvironmental speech data constructed with the same noise and\n1157\tRIR augmentation pipeline as the pretraining data, enabling\n1158\t\n1159\tFollowing standard zero-shot TTS evaluation practice, we\n1160\tassessed the synthesized speech in terms of naturalness, intelligibility, and speaker similarity. Given the environmentaware nature of our task, we further evaluated environmental\n1161\tfidelity to measure how well the synthesized speech reproduces\n1162\tthe target acoustic environment. Both objective metrics and\n1163\tsubjective listening tests were used for evaluation.\n1164\t1) Objective Metrics: Speech intelligibility was assessed by\n1165\tcomputing the word error rate (WER) using Whisper-large-v3\n1166\t[39] to transcribe the synthesized speech and comparing the\n1167\ttranscription against the target text. A lower WER indicates\n1168\thigher intelligibility and more accurate text-speech alignment.\n1169\tSpeaker similarity was quantified by computing the cosine\n1170\tsimilarity between speaker embeddings extracted from the\n1171\tsynthesized speech and the speaker prompt using the WavLMlarge-based speaker verification model [40], denoted as SECS\n1172\t(Speaker Embedding Cosine Similarity). Since speaker verification models are typically trained on data with diverse\n1173\tacoustic conditions, the extracted embeddings are reasonably\n1174\trobust to environmental variations, enabling speaker similarity\n1175\tto be evaluated even when the synthesized speech contains\n1176\tbackground noise or reverberation.\n1177\tSince there are no established objective metrics for evaluating environmental fidelity in environment-aware TTS, we\n1178\tpropose to adopt two complementary metrics for this purpose.\n1179\tContrastive language-audio pretraining (CLAP) similarity [41]\n1180\tmeasures the pairwise cosine similarity between CLAP embeddings of the synthesized speech and the environment\n1181\tprompt. CLAP is trained via contrastive learning on largescale audio-text pairs, and its audio encoder captures high-level\n1182\tacoustic scene attributes such as background sound events\n1183\tand overall acoustic characteristics, rather than relying on\n1184\twaveform-level matching. A higher CLAP similarity indicates\n1185\tbetter environmental consistency at the sample level. We further use the Fréchet audio distance (FAD) [42] computed using\n1186\t4 Audio samples: https://yxlu-0102.github.io/DAIEN-TTS/journal.\n1187\t\n1188\t\fJOURNAL OF LATEX CLASS FILES, VOL. 14, NO. 8, AUGUST 2015\n1189\t\n1190\tVGGish [43] embeddings to evaluate environmental fidelity\n1191\tat the distribution level. FAD computes the Fréchet distance\n1192\tbetween Gaussian distributions fitted to the embeddings of\n1193\tthe synthesized and reference audio sets, providing a holistic\n1194\tmeasure of how well the overall distribution of acoustic\n1195\tenvironments is reproduced. A lower FAD indicates better\n1196\tenvironmental fidelity.\n1197\t2) Subjective Metrics: We conducted three types of listening tests. A mean opinion score (MOS) test was conducted\n1198\tto assess the overall naturalness of the synthesized speech, including both the speech content and acoustic environment, on a\n1199\t1–5 scale with 0.5-point resolution. A speaker similarity MOS\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "7ac83494-635c-4c75-8a11-1d3a2be4f75e", "uuid": "c4bf39f6-87ad-42b7-9aa5-cd53a07bdf3b", "timestamp": "2026-08-05T11:35:43.984Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/daien-tts.txt", "content": "(a) Pre-training on simulated data\n\nECAPA\nTDNN\n\nWR\n\n(b) Fine-tuning on real-world data\n\nF g 2 Ove v ew o he wo s age a n ng o he ex ended DA EN TTS w h he de a ed D T b ock s uc u e shown n he m dd e a Pre ra n ng on\ns mu a ed da a he no sy a ge x1 s cons uc ed om he c ean speech s o he a ge speake a se ec ed no se n and R R h wh e he eve be a on\ncond on s ex ac ed om he speech s o a d e en speake convo ved w h he same h b F ne un ng on rea wor d da a ne he n no h s ava ab e\nns ead he SES modu e sepa a es he speech and no se componen s om ea env onmen a eco d ngs o p ov de he cond ons Snowflake and flame cons\ndeno e ozen and a nab e modu es espec ve y\n\nover he seman c con en and speaker charac er s cs us ng\nes ab shed zero-sho TTS me hods he pr mary cha enge es\nn effec ve y mode ng and con ro ng he acous c env ronmen wh ch s nheren y comp ex and composed of mu p e\nhe erogeneous fac ors\nOn he bas s of he d s nc phys ca proper es expressed\nw h Eq (1) we decompose he acous c env ronmen n o\na me-vary ng componen and me- nvar an componen and\nmode hem w h d fferen represen a ons he me-vary ng\ncomponen s cap ured by a frame- eve represen a on o\npreserve s empora dynam cs wh e he me- nvar an componen s encoded as an u erance- eve embedd ng o reflec s\ng oba charac er s cs The de a s of how hese represen a ons\nare ob a ned and n egra ed n o our TTS framework are\ndescr bed n he fo ow ng sec on\nIII P ROPOSED M ETHOD\nA Sys em Overv ew\nThe arch ec ure of he ex ended DAIEN-TTS s us ra ed\nn F gs 2 and 3 Bu upon F5-TTS [8] a flow-ma ch ng-based\nzero-sho TTS framework ha opera es n he me -spec rogram\ndoma n v a masked aud o nfi ng [7] he sys em n roduces\nwo core componen s o enab e env ronmen -aware genera on\nan SES modu e (F g 3) for d sen ang ng speaker and env ronmen nforma on and env ronmen cond on ng mechan sm n\nhe TTS modu e (F g 2) for con ro ab e recomb na on\nThe SES modu e (Sec III-B) decomposes an env ronmen a\nspeech s gna n o a c ean speech componen no se componen and reverbera on componen wh ch are conver ed\nn o cond ons for he TTS modu e me -spec rograms cspk\nand cno se and an u erance- eve reverbera on embedd ng\n\nSES Modu e\nY\n\nM k ngN\n\nM\n\ny\n\nN\n\nW\n\nY\n\nM k ngN\n\n→\n\nR\n\nW\n\n→\n\nW\n\nY\n\n→\n\nY\n\nF g 3 Ove v ew o he SES modu e w h wo s age no se and eve be a on\nsepa a on\n\ncreverb The TTS modu e (Sec III-C) genera es he arge\nenv ronmen a me -spec rogram cond oned on hese fac ors\na ong w h he ex npu wh ch s subsequen y conver ed o\na waveform by us ng a vocoder\nDur ng ra n ng (Sec III-D) he wo modu es are ra ned\nsepara e y us ng s mu a ed da a w h ground- ru h s gna s\nw h a cross-speaker cond on ng s ra egy o preven speaker\nnforma on eakage When rea -wor d da a are ava ab e he\nex ended DAIEN-TTS can be fur her fine- uned (Sec III-E)\nby connec ng he wo modu es n o a fu d sen ang emen recomb na on p pe ne A nference (Sec III-F) TCFG and\nSNR adap a on enab e ndependen con ro over he speech\nno se and reverbera on componen s\nB SES Modu e\nT me-frequency mask ng s a we -es ab shed echn que\nfor aud o source separa on and speech enhancemen [23]\n[24] where a mask s es ma ed and app ed o he m x ure\nspec rogram o ex rac or suppress arge s gna componen s I\nhas a so been shown o be effec ve for suppress ng convo u ve\n\n\fJOURNAL OF LATEX CLASS FILES, VOL. 14, NO. 8, AUGUST 2015\n\n4\n\ndistortions such as reverberation [25]. Since the downstream\nTTS module operates on mel-spectrograms derived from magnitude spectrograms and does not require phase information,\nour SES module is designed to execute masking in the\nmagnitude spectrogram domain. As illustrated in Fig. 3, the\nSES module adopts a cascaded two-stage design, where the\nfirst stage separates noise and the second stage separates\nreverberation.\nIn the first stage, the input environmental speech y is\nsplit into two components: a reverberant speech component and noise component. This is achieved by transforming y into a magnitude spectrogram |Y| ∈ RF ×L via the\nshort-time Fourier transform, where F and L denote the\nnumber of frequency bins and time frames, respectively. A\nTransformer-based [26] masking network for noise separation\n(MaskingNet-N) then predicts two complementary separation\nmasks WS+R and WN ∈ RF ×L , which are applied to |Y|\nvia element-wise multiplication:\n|YS+R | = |Y| ⊙ WN ,\n\n|YN | = |Y| ⊙ WS+R .\n\n(2)\n\nIn the second stage, the reverberation is further separated from the reverberant speech. Another masking network\n(MaskingNet-R) predicts a reverberation mask WR ∈ RF ×L ,\nand the clean speech magnitude spectrogram is obtained by\n|YS | = |YS+R | ⊙ WR .\n\n(3)\n\nThe SES module thus outputs three disentangled components: |YS |, |YN |, and WR . Note that unlike noise, which\nis additive and can be directly separated from the mixture as\nan independent signal, reverberation is a convolutive effect\ninseparable from the speech content in the spectro-temporal\ndomain. Therefore, rather than attempting to recover the RIR,\nwe use the reverberation mask WR as an implicit representation of the reverberation characteristics, as it encodes the\nspectro-temporal distortion introduced by the room acoustics.\nHow these three components are transformed into conditions\nfor the TTS module is described in the next subsection.\nC. Environment-aware TTS Module\nOur TTS module is built upon F5-TTS [8], which formulates zero-shot TTS as a text-guided speech infilling task [7]\nunder the conditional flow matching (CFM) framework [27].\nGiven a speech mel-spectrogram x1 and its corresponding text\nsequence z, a random span mask m is applied to x1 , where the\nunmasked portion serves as the speaker prompt. Starting from\na sampled Gaussian noise x0 ∼ N (0, I), the noisy input is\nconstructed as ψt (x0 ) = (1−t)x0 +tx1 , where t is a randomly\nsampled flow step. A Diffusion Transformer (DiT) [28] based\nnetwork Vt is trained to predict the velocity field that transforms x0 towards x1 , conditioned on the unmasked portion\n(1−m)⊙x1 and the full text sequence z, which includes both\nthe transcription of the unmasked prompt and the text to be\ngenerated. To extend this framework for environment-aware\ngeneration, we reformulate the task as a disentangled audio\ninfilling problem, where the model simultaneously infills the\nmasked speech and its corresponding acoustic environment,\n\nconditioned on the text and the disentangled speaker and\nenvironment representations.\nThe environment-aware TTS module takes three conditions\nas input: a frame-level speaker condition cspk , frame-level\nnoise condition cnoise , and utterance-level reverberation embedding creverb . In the pretraining stage, the training target\n(y at the bottom of Fig. 2 (a)) is a simulated environmental\nspeech from a clean speech signal s, noise signal n, and RIR\nh, as expressed with Eq. (1). The cspk and cnoise are obtained\nby directly converting s and n into mel-spectrograms. The\ncreverb is extracted by convolving a different speaker’s clean\nspeech s′ with the same RIR h, computing the reverberation\nmask WR from the resulting clean-reverberant pair, and\npassing it through an ECAPA-TDNN [13] encoder. This crossspeaker conditioning strategy ensures that creverb captures\nonly the room acoustic characteristics without leaking the\ntarget speaker’s identity. In the fine-tuning stage (Fig. 2 (b)),\nthe training target is real-world environmental speech, and the\nthree conditions are provided by the SES module applied to\nthe same input.\nDuring training, random span masks of varying lengths\nmspeech and mnoise are independently applied to the disentangled clean speech mel-spectrogram cspk and noise melspectrogram cnoise , respectively. The reverberation embedding\ncreverb is an utterance-level representation, thus does not require masking. During inference, the speaker and environment\nconditions are derived from two separate audio recordings\nthat are generally of different lengths. The clean speech\ncondition is temporally aligned with the text sequence, the\nnoise condition has no such constraint, and the reverberation\nembedding is utterance-level, thus independent of the prompt\nlength. Applying masks of independent lengths during training\nenables the model to accommodate this length mismatch at\ninference, avoiding truncation or padding of the environment\ncondition that could lead to loss of environment information.\nThe three conditions are injected into the DiT blocks\nthrough different mechanisms, as depicted in the middle of\nFig. 2. The unmasked speaker condition (1 − mspeech ) ⊙ cspk\nis concatenated with the text embedding z and the noisy input\nψt (x0 ) along the feature dimension, following the original F5TTS design. For the environment conditions, the reverberation\nembedding creverb is first repeated along the frame dimension\nto match the length of the noise condition then added to the\nunmasked noise condition (1−mnoise )⊙cnoise to form a unified environment representation. This combined environment\ncondition is introduced via a multi-head cross-attention layer\ninserted into each DiT block, enabling the model to jointly\nattend to frame-level noise patterns and global reverberation\ncharacteristics. With these conditions, the model learns to\nreconstruct the masked environmental speech mel-spectrogram\nmspeech ⊙ x1 , including both the personalized speech content\nand its corresponding acoustic environment.\nD. Pretraining on Simulated Data\nAs illustrated in Fig. 2 (a), we construct simulated environmental speech by mixing clean speech with noise and RIRs at\nvarious SNR levels, providing paired data with known groundtruth components for supervised training of both modules.\n\n\fJOURNAL OF LATEX CLASS FILES, VOL. 14, NO. 8, AUGUST 2015\n\n5\n\nEnvironmental\nPersonalized Speech\n\nThe SES module is pretrained independently with a combination of magnitude spectrogram and mel-spectrogram reconstruction losses over all three separated components:\nLSES = Lmag + Lmel ,\n\nTarget Mel\n\nDiscard\n\nODE Solver\n\n(4)\nvt\n\nSway Sampled\nTime Step t\n\nwhere\n\n+ |ŶN | − |YN |\n\n2\n2\n\n2\n\n<latexit sha1_base64=\"/gXV1hjf8BVEJzoOhCDiRduwqD8=\"></latexit>\n\nN → DiT Blocks\n\n+ |ŶS | − |YS |\n\n2\n2\n\n<latexit sha1_base64=\"7lfWxdcM0w6+1+eZt9k6zXGaw+c=\"></latexit>\n\n(5)\n\nC\n\nx0 → N (I, 0)\n<latexit sha1_base64=\"8gaUj6IWXvmma+m+gaBmI4bPEX4=\"></latexit>\n\n,\nzref\nText\n\nzref ·gen T H E\n\n1\n\nGaussian Noise\n\nzgen\n\n<latexit sha1_base64=\"qEuDxMgdEAJgqOToVwRjbWL1g4w=\"></latexit>\n\n<latexit sha1_base64=\"15iOQdwaPD172saHm86DzPRdkHo=\"></latexit>\n\nP R I C E\n\n<latexit sha1_base64=\"UN80LFVIE5ruU8TafbjeaLPpNB4=\"></latexit>\n\nLmel = Mel(|ŶS+R |) − Mel(|YS+R |)\n\n<latexit sha1_base64=\"qsk3mpAkyyHYS5S47vRny3Ok9Hc=\"></latexit>\n\n<latexit sha1_base64=\"pC5O0yYlY6G1Vk4VdWIJxXDJLx0=\"></latexit>\n\nConvNeXt V2\n\n2\n\nECAPATDNN\n\ncreverb\ncnoise\n\n<latexit sha1_base64=\"sk80zL9UtHmK7/DxTVagmPDPbv4=\"></latexit>\n\n<latexit sha1_base64=\"wIuqdg4I+aI5/95yudiXsxGS9Ag=\"></latexit>\n\nLmag = |ŶS+R | − |YS+R |\n\nVocoder\n\ncspk\n\nH E … H I G H . <F> <F> <F>\n\nTo Generate\n\n<latexit sha1_base64=\"0ceIEsSVpBXYQMRm4fCYfQ23Fe4=\"></latexit>\n\n+ Mel(|ŶS |) − Mel(|YS |) ,\ncnoise\nTo Generate\n1\n1\n(6)\nN\nN\n|Yspk\n|\n|Yenv\n|\n|Ŷ| and |Y| denote the predicted and corresponding groundR\nR\nS\nS\nWspk\nWenv\nSES Module\n|Yspk\n|\n|Yenv\n|\nSES Module\ntruth magnitude spectrogram, and Mel(·) denotes the log-melspectrogram conversion via a mel filter bank.\nyspk\nyenv\nSpk. Prompt\nEnv. Prompt\nThe TTS module is pretrained separately using the conditional flow matching objective:\nFig. 4. Inference process of the extended DAIEN-TTS. Given text z, a speaker\n\u0002\nyspk , and environment prompt yenv , the SES module extracts the\nLCFM = Vt ψt (x0 )|z, (1 − mspeech ) ⊙ cspk , (1 − mnoise ) prompt\nspeaker and environment conditions from the speaker prompt and environment\nprompt, respectively, for generation.\n2\n\u0001\n\u0003\n⊙ cnoise , creverb − (x1 − x0 ) ⊙ mspeech ,\n+ Mel(|ŶN |) − Mel(|YN |)\n\n<latexit sha1_base64=\"qsk3mpAkyyHYS5S47vRny3Ok9Hc=\"></latexit>\n\nMel\n\n<latexit sha1_base64=\"Xos59SmyrSNElNhb0Ezl91IGQmc=\"></latexit>\n\n<latexit sha1_base64=\"zaPgjQiWPrJGYOwnNkEAHuU0fb0=\"></latexit>\n\nMel\n\n<latexit sha1_base64=\"c5QZum/rX4nM0B5E2MMU5748Pqc=\"></latexit>\n\n<latexit sha1_base64=\"7YIXNO6512D+nK/RPSDQPT6w8Vg=\"></latexit>\n\n<latexit sha1_base64=\"pTdOpy+3in/Pgw3ORp1gwIIUcXk=\"></latexit>\n\n<latexit sha1_base64=\"+06IGHTryUCydNvRyB30dPoiiJE=\"></latexit>\n\n<latexit sha1_base64=\"ZQ+nMskbG8V6+7ik+9luU1eOItc=\"></latexit>\n\n<latexit sha1_base64=\"AcQ6Sc1tk8nnuQDe9qUkOWP73dY=\"></latexit>\n\n2\n\n(7)\n\nwhere Vt (·) is the learned velocity field. As described in\nSec. III-C, the reverberation mask used for extracting creverb\nis computed from a different speaker’s utterance to prevent\nspeaker information leakage. Specifically, for each training\nsample, we randomly select an utterance from a different\nspeaker, truncate or repeat-pad it to match the target length,\nand convolve it with the same RIR applied to the original\nutterance. This ensures that the reverberation mask retains\nonly the room acoustic characteristics while discarding the\ntarget speaker’s identity. Noise and reverberation augmentations are independently applied to each clean speech sample\nwith probabilities pnoise and prir , respectively, resulting in\nfour possible training conditions: clean speech, noisy speech,\nreverberant speech, and noisy-reverberant speech. This ensures\nthat the model learns to handle diverse environmental conditions while maintaining robust text-speech alignment from the\nclean speech cases.\nE. Fine-tuning on Real-world Data\nAs illustrated in Fig. 2 (b), to bridge the domain gap\nbetween simulated training data and real-world recordings,\nwe fine-tune the extended DAIEN-TTS on real-world environmental speech data by connecting the SES and TTS modules\ninto a full pipeline. Unlike pretraining where the TTS module\nreceives ground-truth conditions, during fine-tuning the three\nconditions cspk , cnoise , and creverb are directly provided\nby the SES module applied to the real-world environmental\nspeech. The TTS module is then trained to reconstruct the\nsame input environmental speech using LCFM .\nSeveral components are frozen during fine-tuning. The SES\nmodule is frozen as real-world environmental speech lacks\n\npaired clean speech references, making supervised optimization of LSES infeasible. The text encoder is frozen to preserve\nthe text-speech alignment acquired during pretraining. The\nECAPA-TDNN reverb encoder is frozen because ground-truth\nRIRs are unavailable for real-world recordings, precluding\nthe application of the cross-speaker conditioning strategy;\nupdating the reverb encoder without this constraint would\nrisk speaker information leakage through the environment\nbranch. The remaining parameters, including the DiT blocks\nand environment cross-attention layers, are updated to adapt\nto the acoustic characteristics of real-world environments.\nF. Inference\nThe inference process is illustrated in Fig. 4. Given a\nspeaker audio prompt yspk and environment audio prompt\nyenv , the SES module is applied to each prompt separately:\nS\nfrom yspk , we extract the clean speech component |Yspk\n| and\nconvert it to the speaker condition cspk ; from yenv , we extract\nN\nR\nthe noise component |Yenv\n| and reverberation mask Wenv\n,\nwhich are converted to cnoise and creverb , respectively. With\nthe transcription of the speaker prompt zref and the text to\nbe generated zgen , we concatenate them as zref ·gen and use\nan ordinary differential equation (ODE) solver to generate the\ntarget mel-spectrogram. Starting from sampled Gaussian noise\nx0 ∼ N (0, I), the ODE solver integrates towards the target\nx1 according to\n\u0001\ndψt (x0 )/dt = Vt ψt (x0 ), zref ·gen , cspk , cnoise , creverb .\n(8)\nAfter generation, the prompt region corresponding to zref is\ndiscarded, and the remaining generated mel-spectrogram is\nconverted to a waveform by using the vocoder.\n\n\fJOURNAL OF LATEX CLASS FILES, VOL. 14, NO. 8, AUGUST 2015\n\n6\n\nThe above inference procedure generates environmental\nspeech with the specified speaker and environment conditions\nbut offers limited control over the contribution of each environmental component to the synthesized output. To enhance\nthe controllability at inference, we further introduce two\ncomplementary strategies: TCFG for independently adjusting\nthe strength of the speech, noise, and reverberation components, and SNR adaptation for aligning the noise level of the\nsynthesized speech with that of the environment prompt.\n1) Triple Classifier-free Guidance: F5-TTS uses CFG [29]\nto balance generation fidelity and diversity by treating the text\nand speaker conditions as a unified guidance term. In our prior\nwork [1], we adopted a DCFG [16] mechanism to separately\nguide the speech and environment components. In this work,\nsince the environment condition is further decomposed into\nnoise and reverberation, we extended DCFG to TCFG for\nindependent control over the speech, noise, and reverberation\ncomponents. Let cspeech = {z, cspk } denote the unified speech\ncondition combining the text sequence and speaker condition.\nThe TCFG is formulated as\nVt,TCFG = Vt (ψt (x0 ), cspeech , cnoise , creverb )\n\n+ αspeech [Vt (ψt (x0 ), cspeech , ∅, ∅) − Vt (ψt (x0 ), ∅, ∅, ∅)]\n+ αnoise [Vt (ψt (x0 ), ∅, cnoise , ∅) − Vt (ψt (x0 ), ∅, ∅, ∅)]\n\n+ αreverb [Vt (ψt (x0 ), ∅, ∅, creverb ) − Vt (ψt (x0 ), ∅, ∅, ∅)] ,\n(9)\nwhere ∅ denotes the null condition, and αspeech , αnoise , and\nαreverb are the guidance scales for the speech, noise, and\nreverberation components, respectively. The derivation follows\nnaturally from the DCFG formulation [16] by extending to\nthree independent conditions. Adjusting these three scales independently enables fine-grained control over the contribution\nof each component to the synthesized environmental speech.\n2) SNR Adaptation: The SNR of the environment prompt\ncharacterizes the relative prominence of the background noise\nin the acoustic scene, thus an important attribute to be preserved in the synthesized speech. However, since the speaker\nand environment conditions are extracted from separate audio\nprompts with potentially different energy levels, a direct combination of these conditions may result in an SNR discrepancy\nbetween the synthesized speech and environment prompt.\nTo mitigate this, we introduce an SNR adaptation strategy\nthat rescales the noise condition such that the SNR of the\nsynthesized speech is consistent with that of the environment\nprompt.\nConsidering the environmental speech y is composed of\nits reverberant speech component yS+R and noise component\nyN , such that y = yS+R + yN , the SNR can be expressed as\nSNR = 10 log10\n\n∥YS+R ∥22\n∥yS+R ∥22\n=\n10\nlog\n,\n10\n∥yN ∥22\n∥YN ∥22\n\n(10)\n\nwhere the second equality follows from Parseval’s theorem.\nTo match the SNR of the synthesized speech with that of the\nenvironment prompt, we require\nS+R 2\n∥Yspk\n∥2\n\nS+R 2\n∥Yenv\n∥2\n10 log10\n= 10 log10\n,\n2\nN\nN\n∥Yenv · Scale∥2\n∥Yenv ∥22\n\nS+R\nwhere Yspk\ndenotes the separated reverberant speech magniS+R\nN\ntude spectrogram of the speaker prompt, and Yenv\nand Yenv\ndenote the separated reverberant speech and noise magnitude\nspectrograms of the environment prompt, respectively. Solving\nfor the scaling factor yields\ns\nS+R 2\n∥Yspk\n∥2\n.\n(12)\nScale =\nS+R 2\n∥Yenv ∥2\nN\nDuring inference, |Yenv\n| is multiplied by this factor prior to\nmel-spectrogram conversion, ensuring that the resulting noise\ncondition reflects the target SNR of the environment prompt\nrelative to the speech energy of the speaker prompt.\n\nIV. E XPERIMENTAL S ETUP\nA. Datasets\nOur experiments involve two types of data: simulated\nenvironmental speech for pretraining and evaluation under\ncontrolled conditions, and real-world environmental speech for\nfine-tuning and evaluation under realistic conditions.\n1) Simulated Data: For pretraining, we used the LibriTTS\ncorpus [30], which contains 580 hours of high-quality English\nspeech data. To construct simulated environmental speech, the\nclean speech was mixed with noise samples from the DNSChallenge noise set [31], which comprises approximately 180\nhours of environmental audio sourced from AudioSet [32] and\nFreeSound [33]. RIRs were sampled from the DNS-Challenge\nRIR set 1 , containing 20,248 samples. The SNR for noise\nmixing was uniformly sampled between −5 and 15 dB, and\nthe noise and RIR augmentations were independently applied\nwith probabilities pnoise and prir , both set to 0.5.\nFor evaluation under simulated conditions, we constructed\na test set based on the Seed-TTS test-en set2 , which originally\nprovides the text to be synthesized, a speaker reference audio\nwith its transcription, and a corresponding human-recorded\nground truth for each sample. To evaluate environment-aware\nTTS, we extended each sample with an environment reference\naudio by randomly selecting an utterance from a different\nspeaker. Both the speaker and environment reference audios\nwere augmented with independently sampled noise and RIRs,\nwith SNRs uniformly distributed between 0 and 20 dB. The\nnoise clips were sourced from SoundBible3 and the RIRs\nfrom the SRIRACHA dataset [34]. To construct the ground\ntruth for the environmental personalized speech, the original\nclean ground-truth recording was first convolved with the\nsame RIR applied to the environment reference audio. For\nthe noise component, since the TTS model is expected to\ncontinue the background noise from the environment prompt,\nthe noise signal appended to the ground truth was taken from\nthe segment immediately following the portion used in the\nenvironment reference audio, preserving temporal continuity.\nAll data were constructed at a sampling rate of 24 kHz.\n1 https://www.openslr.org/26/, https://www.openslr.org/28/\n\n(11)\n\n2 https://github.com/BytedanceSpeech/seed-tts-eval\n3 https://soundbible.com\n\n\fJOURNAL OF LATEX CLASS FILES, VOL. 14, NO. 8, AUGUST 2015\n\n2) Real-world Data: For fine-tuning, we used the TITWHard subset of the TITW (TTS in the Wild) dataset [35],\nwhich is derived from VoxCeleb 1 [36] through an automated\ntranscription, segmentation, and selection pipeline, containing\n273,493 utterances totaling 184.57 hours of English speech\nrecorded under diverse real-world acoustic conditions. Since\nthe original TITW data are recorded at 16 kHz, all audio\nwas upsampled to 24 kHz to match the sampling rate of the\npretraining data.\nFor evaluation under real-world conditions, we used text\nfrom the LibriTTS test-clean set, while the speaker and environment reference audios were selected from the TITW-Hard\ntest set. To ensure distinct acoustic conditions between the\ntwo references, we scored all TITW test samples using DNSMOS [37] and selected speaker prompts from samples with\nbackground-noise quality (BAK) scores above 3.5, indicating\nrelatively clean recordings, and environment prompts from\nsamples with BAK scores below 2.5, indicating prominent\nbackground noise or reverberation.\n\n7\n\nit to reconstruct environmental speech waveforms from melspectrograms. 4\nC. Baseline and Compared Systems\nWe evaluated the following systems in our experiments:\n• F5-TTS [8]: The base F5-TTS model trained on the\nLibriTTS dataset for 600k steps, representing a standard\nzero-shot TTS system without environment awareness.\n• DAIEN-TTS-N [1]: The conference version of DAIENTTS, which only models background noise without reverberation and trained exclusively on simulated data.\n• DAIEN-TTS-NR: The extended DAIEN-TTS that jointly\nmodels background noise and reverberation, pretrained on\nsimulated data.\n• DAIEN-TTS-FT: The extended DAIEN-TTS fine-tuned\non real-world speech data from the TITW-Hard dataset.\nFor a fair comparison, all systems share the same LibriTTS\npretraining data and Vocos vocoder.\nD. Evaluation Metrics\n\nB. Model Configuration\nIn the SES module, both MaskingNet-N and MaskingNet-R\nconsist of an input convolutional layer, 8 Transformer blocks\nwith 16 attention heads, an embedding dimension of 1024,\nand feed-forward network (FFN) dimension of 2048, followed\nby output convolutional layers. For the TTS module, we\nadopted the same DiT architecture as F5-TTS, with each DiT\nblock inserted by a cross-attention layer configured with 16\nattention heads for environment conditioning. The ECAPATDNN [13] encoder for reverberation embedding extraction\nis jointly trained with the TTS module during pretraining,\nand frozen during fine-tuning to prevent speaker information\nleakage. All audio was represented as 100-dimensional log\nmel-filterbank features at a sampling rate of 24 kHz with a\nhop length of 256.\nBoth SES and TTS modules were pretrained for 600k steps\non 4 NVIDIA A100 80G GPUs, using the AdamW optimizer\nwith a peak learning rate of 7.5 × 10−5 , linearly warmed up\nfor 20k steps and linearly decayed thereafter, and a batch size\nof 120,000 audio frames. During training, a random 70 to\n100% of mel frames are independently masked for both the\nspeech and noise conditions, while the reverberation condition\nis always provided in full. For CFG training, the speech, noise,\nand reverberation conditions are each independently dropped\nwith a probability of 0.3, and the speech condition together\nwith the text input is jointly dropped with an additional\nprobability of 0.2. For fine-tuning on real-world data, the\nlearning rate was reduced to 1 × 10−5 with a warm-up of\n2k steps, and the system was fine-tuned for 50k steps. During\ninference, the TCFG guidance scales were set to αspeech = 1,\nαnoise = 3, and αreverb = 6 unless otherwise specified, and\nthe number of function evaluations for the ODE solver was\nset to 32. For the vocoder, we trained a Vocos [38] model on\nenvironmental speech data constructed with the same noise and\nRIR augmentation pipeline as the pretraining data, enabling\n\nFollowing standard zero-shot TTS evaluation practice, we\nassessed the synthesized speech in terms of naturalness, intelligibility, and speaker similarity. Given the environmentaware nature of our task, we further evaluated environmental\nfidelity to measure how well the synthesized speech reproduces\nthe target acoustic environment. Both objective metrics and\nsubjective listening tests were used for evaluation.\n1) Objective Metrics: Speech intelligibility was assessed by\ncomputing the word error rate (WER) using Whisper-large-v3\n[39] to transcribe the synthesized speech and comparing the\ntranscription against the target text. A lower WER indicates\nhigher intelligibility and more accurate text-speech alignment.\nSpeaker similarity was quantified by computing the cosine\nsimilarity between speaker embeddings extracted from the\nsynthesized speech and the speaker prompt using the WavLMlarge-based speaker verification model [40], denoted as SECS\n(Speaker Embedding Cosine Similarity). Since speaker verification models are typically trained on data with diverse\nacoustic conditions, the extracted embeddings are reasonably\nrobust to environmental variations, enabling speaker similarity\nto be evaluated even when the synthesized speech contains\nbackground noise or reverberation.\nSince there are no established objective metrics for evaluating environmental fidelity in environment-aware TTS, we\npropose to adopt two complementary metrics for this purpose.\nContrastive language-audio pretraining (CLAP) similarity [41]\nmeasures the pairwise cosine similarity between CLAP embeddings of the synthesized speech and the environment\nprompt. CLAP is trained via contrastive learning on largescale audio-text pairs, and its audio encoder captures high-level\nacoustic scene attributes such as background sound events\nand overall acoustic characteristics, rather than relying on\nwaveform-level matching. A higher CLAP similarity indicates\nbetter environmental consistency at the sample level. We further use the Fréchet audio distance (FAD) [42] computed using\n4 Audio samples: https://yxlu-0102.github.io/DAIEN-TTS/journal.\n\n\fJOURNAL OF LATEX CLASS FILES, VOL. 14, NO. 8, AUGUST 2015\n\nVGGish [43] embeddings to evaluate environmental fidelity\nat the distribution level. FAD computes the Fréchet distance\nbetween Gaussian distributions fitted to the embeddings of\nthe synthesized and reference audio sets, providing a holistic\nmeasure of how well the overall distribution of acoustic\nenvironments is reproduced. A lower FAD indicates better\nenvironmental fidelity.\n2) Subjective Metrics: We conducted three types of listening tests. A mean opinion score (MOS) test was conducted\nto assess the overall naturalness of the synthesized speech, including both the speech content and acoustic environment, on a\n1–5 scale with 0.5-point resolution. A speaker similarity MOS", "numLines": 700, "startLine": 500, "totalLines": 1961}}}
