{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a4ede8c055d56735", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1200\t(SSMOS) test was conducted by providing the speaker prompt\n1201\tas a reference and asking listeners to rate how closely the synthesized speech matches the target speaker’s voice, focusing on\n1202\tspeaker identity while ignoring the acoustic environment. An\n1203\tenvironment similarity MOS (ESMOS) test was conducted by\n1204\tproviding the environment prompt as a reference and asking\n1205\tlisteners to rate the consistency in terms of background noise,\n1206\tnon-speech sound events, and reverberation, while ignoring\n1207\tspeaker identity and speech content. All listening tests were\n1208\tcrowd-sourced via Amazon Mechanical Turk with more than\n1209\t30 native English-speaking raters, each rating 20 samples per\n1210\ttest. We report the mean scores with 95% confidence intervals\n1211\t(CI).\n1212\tV. R ESULTS AND A NALYSIS\n1213\tA. Environment-robust Generation\n1214\tWe first evaluated the ability of each system to synthesize\n1215\tclean personalized speech when given a silence environment\n1216\tprompt, under two speaker prompt conditions: clean and simulated noisy-reverberant. In this scenario, the system is expected\n1217\tto disentangle the environmental factors from the speaker\n1218\tprompt and produce clean, natural speech. For the MOS test\n1219\tin this scenario, listeners were asked to evaluate both the\n1220\tnaturalness and acoustic cleanliness of the synthesized speech,\n1221\twith any residual noise or reverberation artifacts considered as\n1222\tquality degradation. For the SSMOS test, the clean version of\n1223\tthe speaker prompt was provided as the reference since paired\n1224\tclean-environmental speech is available in the simulated test\n1225\tset. Table I presents the evaluation results, where the groundtruth recordings and their vocoder-resynthesized versions serve\n1226\tas upper bounds.\n1227\tUnder the clean speaker prompt condition, all TTS systems achieved comparable performance across all metrics.\n1228\tThe DAIEN-TTS variants outperformed F5-TTS in WER and\n1229\tSECS, which can be attributed to the data augmentation effect\n1230\tintroduced by training on paired clean-environmental speech\n1231\tdata. The MOS and SSMOS scores of all systems were\n1232\tclose, with overlapping confidence intervals, indicating that\n1233\tthe additional environment modeling in DAIEN-TTS does not\n1234\tdegrade synthesis quality when clean prompts are provided.\n1235\tUnder the simulated noisy-reverberant speaker prompt condition, clear performance differences emerged among the\n1236\tsystems. F5-TTS, which is trained exclusively on clean speech,\n1237\texhibited substantial degradation in both intelligibility and\n1238\tspeaker similarity compared with the clean prompt condition, confirming the sensitivity of ICL-based TTS to adverse\n1239\t\n1240\t8\n1241\t\n1242\tTABLE I\n1243\tO BJECTIVE AND SUBJECTIVE EVALUATION RESULTS OF\n1244\tENVIRONMENT- ROBUST GENERATION ON THE S EED -TTS TEST- EN SET\n1245\tSystem\n1246\t\n1247\tWER (%) ↓\n1248\t\n1249\tSECS ↑\n1250\t\n1251\tMOS (CI) ↑\n1252\t\n1253\tSSMOS (CI) ↑\n1254\t\n1255\tClean Speaker Prompt\n1256\tGround Truth\n1257\tVocoder\n1258\t\n1259\t2.14\n1260\t2.18\n1261\t\n1262\t0.734\n1263\t0.698\n1264\t\n1265\t3.91 (± 0.08)\n1266\t–\n1267\t\n1268\t3.52 (± 0.09)\n1269\t–\n1270\t\n1271\tF5-TTS\n1272\tDAIEN-TTS-N\n1273\tDAIEN-TTS-NR\n1274\t\n1275\t2.30\n1276\t2.07\n1277\t2.03\n1278\t\n1279\t0.590\n1280\t0.617\n1281\t0.628\n1282\t\n1283\t3.86 (± 0.08)\n1284\t3.91 (± 0.08)\n1285\t3.90 (± 0.08)\n1286\t\n1287\t3.46 (± 0.09)\n1288\t3.49 (± 0.08)\n1289\t3.48 (± 0.08)\n1290\t\n1291\tSimulated Noisy-reverberant Speaker Prompt\n1292\tGround Truth\n1293\tVocoder\n1294\tF5-TTS\n1295\tDAIEN-TTS-N\n1296\tDAIEN-TTS-NR\n1297\t\n1298\t2.14\n1299\t2.18\n1300\t2.80\n1301\t2.75\n1302\t2.24\n1303\t\n1304\t0.734\n1305\t0.698\n1306\t0.317\n1307\t0.380\n1308\t0.486\n1309\t\n1310\t3.95 (± 0.07)\n1311\t–\n1312\t3.75 (± 0.08)\n1313\t3.77 (± 0.07)\n1314\t3.91 (± 0.08)\n1315\t\n1316\t3.43 (± 0.08)\n1317\t–\n1318\t3.19 (± 0.09)\n1319\t3.30 (± 0.08)\n1320\t3.37 (± 0.08)\n1321\t\n1322\tacoustic conditions in the speaker prompt. DAIEN-TTS-N\n1323\tshowed a similar trend, as its noise-only separation module\n1324\tcannot adequately remove the reverberation component from\n1325\tthe speaker prompt. In contrast, DAIEN-TTS-NR achieved\n1326\tthe best performance across all metrics, with its MOS score\n1327\tcomparable to the ground-truth reference, demonstrating that\n1328\tjoint modeling of noise and reverberation is essential for robust\n1329\tsynthesis under adverse acoustic conditions.\n1330\t\n1331\tB. Environment-aware Generation\n1332\tWe next evaluated the ability of each system to synthesize environmental personalized speech that reproduces the\n1333\tacoustic environment specified by the environment prompt.\n1334\tExperiments were conducted under two conditions: simulated\n1335\tspeaker and environment prompts and real-world prompts.\n1336\tIn contrast to the environment-robust scenario, listeners in\n1337\tthe MOS test were instructed to evaluate the overall naturalness of the environmental speech, including both the speech\n1338\tand acoustic environment, without penalizing the presence\n1339\tof background noise and reverberation. For the SSMOS test\n1340\tunder the simulated condition, the clean version of the speaker\n1341\tprompt was provided as the reference since paired cleanenvironmental speech is available. Under the real-world condition, the original speaker prompt was used as the reference,\n1342\tas paired clean recordings are unavailable. Table II presents\n1343\tthe evaluation results.\n1344\t1) Simulated Prompts: Under the simulated prompt condition, the ground-truth recordings and their vocoderresynthesized versions serve as upper bounds. Note that WER\n1345\tin this scenario should be interpreted with caution. The\n1346\tground-truth environmental speech already exhibits a WER\n1347\tof 15.41%, significantly higher than the 2.14% observed for\n1348\tclean speech in Table I, indicating that the ASR system is\n1349\tsubstantially affected by the presence of noise and reverberation. Similarly, higher WERs for the environment-aware\n1350\tsystems may reflect the expected presence of background noise\n1351\tand reverberation in the synthesized speech, rather than a\n1352\tdegradation in synthesis quality. F5-TTS achieved the lowest\n1353\tWER as it lacks an environment conditioning mechanism and\n1354\tdoes not actively reproduce the target acoustic environment.\n1355\t\n1356\t\fJOURNAL OF LATEX CLASS FILES, VOL. 14, NO. 8, AUGUST 2015\n1357\t\n1358\t9\n1359\t\n1360\tTABLE II\n1361\tO BJECTIVE AND SUBJECTIVE EVALUATION RESULTS OF ENVIRONMENT- AWARE GENERATION ON THE S EED -TTS TEST- EN SET ( SIMULATED PROMPTS )\n1362\tAND TITW TEST SET ( REAL - WORLD PROMPTS )\n1363\tSystem\n1364\t\n1365\tWER (%)\n1366\t\n1367\tSECS ↑\n1368\t\n1369\tCLAP SIM ↑\n1370\t\n1371\tFAD (VGGish) ↓\n1372\t\n1373\tMOS (CI) ↑\n1374\t\n1375\tSSMOS (CI) ↑\n1376\t\n1377\tESMOS (CI) ↑\n1378\t\n1379\tSimulated Noisy-reverberant Prompts\n1380\tGround Truth\n1381\tVocoder\n1382\t\n1383\t15.41\n1384\t15.80\n1385\t\n1386\t0.530\n1387\t0.493\n1388\t\n1389\t0.851\n1390\t0.848\n1391\t\n1392\t0.10\n1393\t0.13\n1394\t\n1395\t3.40 (± 0.08)\n1396\t–\n1397\t\n1398\t3.58 (± 0.09)\n1399\t–\n1400\t\n1401\t3.59 (± 0.07)\n1402\t–\n1403\t\n1404\tF5-TTS\n1405\tDAIEN-TTS-N\n1406\tDAIEN-TTS-NR\n1407\t\n1408\t2.80\n1409\t10.85\n1410\t13.05\n1411\t\n1412\t0.317\n1413\t0.325\n1414\t0.361\n1415\t\n1416\t0.685\n1417\t0.743\n1418\t0.770\n1419\t\n1420\t8.56\n1421\t5.73\n1422\t2.11\n1423\t\n1424\t3.19 (± 0.08)\n1425\t3.27 (± 0.08)\n1426\t3.38 (± 0.08)\n1427\t\n1428\t3.41 (± 0.09)\n1429\t3.48 (± 0.09)\n1430\t3.53 (± 0.08)\n1431\t\n1432\t3.27 (± 0.09)\n1433\t3.45 (± 0.08)\n1434\t3.55 (± 0.08)\n1435\t\n1436\tF5-TTS\n1437\tDAIEN-TTS-N\n1438\tDAIEN-TTS-NR\n1439\tDAIEN-TTS-FT\n1440\t\n1441\t12.42\n1442\t16.07\n1443\t10.05\n1444\t10.65\n1445\t\n1446\t0.481\n1447\t0.447\n1448\t0.457\n1449\t0.486\n1450\t\n1451\t0.588\n1452\t0.618\n1453\t0.612\n1454\t0.634\n1455\t\n1456\t3.82 (± 0.08)\n1457\t3.71 (± 0.09)\n1458\t3.74 (± 0.08)\n1459\t3.84 (± 0.09)\n1460\t\n1461\t3.46 (± 0.09)\n1462\t3.55 (± 0.08)\n1463\t3.58 (± 0.08)\n1464\t3.62 (± 0.08)\n1465\t\n1466\tReal-world Environmental Prompts\n1467\t\n1468\tAmong the environment-aware systems, DAIEN-TTS-N\n1469\tpartially reproduced the background noise but lacked reverberation modeling, resulting in moderate environmental\n1470\tfidelity (CLAP: 0.743, FAD: 5.73). DAIEN-TTS-NR achieved\n1471\tthe best environmental fidelity (CLAP: 0.770, FAD: 2.11),\n1472\tsubstantially narrowing the gap to the vocoder upper bound\n1473\t(FAD: 0.13), while also attaining the highest speaker similarity\n1474\t(SECS: 0.361) among all TTS systems.\n1475\tThe subjective evaluation results further corroborate these\n1476\tfindings. DAIEN-TTS-NR achieved the highest scores across\n1477\tall three perceptual dimensions (MOS: 3.38, SSMOS: 3.53,\n1478\tESMOS: 3.55), with its ESMOS approaching the groundtruth reference (3.59), indicating that the reconstructed acoustic environment is perceptually close to the target. F5-TTS\n1479\treceived the lowest MOS (3.19) and ESMOS (3.27), as it is\n1480\ttrained on clean speech and struggles to faithfully reconstruct\n1481\tthe heavy noise and reverberation present in the speaker\n1482\tprompt, resulting in degraded environmental quality despite\n1483\tpartially preserving some background characteristics. These\n1484\tresults confirm that joint modeling of noise and reverberation\n1485\tsignificantly improves the fidelity of environment reconstruction in both objective and subjective evaluations.\n1486\t2) Real-world Prompts: Under the real-world prompt condition, we further included the fine-tuned variant DAIEN-TTSFT to evaluate the effectiveness of real-world data adaptation.\n1487\tNote that the WERs under the real-world condition were\n1488\tgenerally higher than those under the simulated condition for\n1489\tall systems, partly because the transcriptions of the real-world\n1490\tspeaker prompts were obtained via ASR and may contain\n1491\terrors, which propagate through the text input and affect the\n1492\tsynthesized speech.\n1493\tAs expected, F5-TTS yielded the lowest CLAP similarity\n1494\t(0.588) and highest FAD (8.37), as it lacks the ability to reproduce acoustic environments by design. However, it achieved\n1495\ta relatively high MOS (3.40) and the highest SSMOS (3.82)\n1496\tunder this condition, as the speaker prompts were selected to\n1497\tbe relatively clean (DNSMOS BAK > 3.5), resulting in higher\n1498\tspeech quality despite poor environment reconstruction.\n1499\tDAIEN-TTS-N achieved higher environmental fidelity\n1500\t(CLAP: 0.618, FAD: 4.92), but its noise-only separation\n1501\tmodule struggled with the complex acoustic conditions in\n1502\t\n1503\t8.37\n1504\t4.92\n1505\t5.33\n1506\t3.62\n1507\t\n1508\t3.40 (± 0.08)\n1509\t3.29 (± 0.08)\n1510\t3.34 (± 0.08)\n1511\t3.38 (± 0.08)\n1512\t\n1513\treal-world recordings, leading to noticeable degradation in\n1514\tspeech intelligibility. The additional reverberation modeling in\n1515\tDAIEN-TTS-NR did not yield a clear advantage over DAIENTTS-N in environmental fidelity under this condition. An\n1516\tanalysis of the TITW test samples revealed that the acoustic\n1517\tenvironments are predominantly characterized by background\n1518\tnoise, with reverberation playing a less prominent role, which\n1519\tlimits the benefit of joint noise-reverberation modeling.\n1520\tAfter fine-tuning on real-world data, DAIEN-TTS-FT\n1521\tachieved improvements across all metrics, attaining the\n1522\tbest speaker similarity (SECS: 0.486), environmental fidelity\n1523\t(CLAP: 0.634, FAD: 3.62), and subjective scores (MOS: 3.38,\n1524\tSSMOS: 3.84, ESMOS: 3.62), demonstrating the effectiveness\n1525\tof the two-stage training strategy in bridging the simulatedto-real domain gap.\n1526\tC. Analysis Experiment\n1527\t1) Effect of Environment Conditions: To further investigate\n1528\tthe behavior of each system under different types of acoustic environments, we evaluated environment-aware generation\n1529\twith noise-only and reverb-only environment prompts on the\n1530\tsimulated test set. Table III presents the results, complementing the noisy-reverberant results in Table II.\n1531\tUnder the noise-only condition, F5-TTS yielded the lowest\n1532\tCLAP similarity and highest FAD, consistent with its inability\n1533\tto reproduce acoustic environments. Both DAIEN-TTS-N and\n1534\tDAIEN-TTS-NR achieved substantially better environmental\n1535\tfidelity, with DAIEN-TTS-N attaining slightly higher CLAP\n1536\tsimilarity as it is specifically designed for noise-only modeling. The marginal difference between the two systems indicates that the additional reverberation modeling in DAIENTTS-NR does not significantly interfere with noise disentanglement and reconstruction in noise-dominated scenarios.\n1537\tUnder the reverb-only condition, a substantial performance\n1538\tgap emerged. DAIEN-TTS-N, which lacks reverberation modeling, exhibited poor environmental fidelity (FAD: 5.06), as\n1539\tits noise-only separation module fails to properly handle\n1540\treverberant speech. In contrast, DAIEN-TTS-NR achieved\n1541\tnotably better environmental fidelity (FAD: 2.86) and speaker\n1542\tsimilarity (SECS: 0.400 vs. 0.317), confirming the necessity\n1543\tof explicit reverberation modeling.\n1544\t\n1545\t\fJOURNAL OF LATEX CLASS FILES, VOL. 14, NO. 8, AUGUST 2015\n1546\t\n1547\t10\n1548\t\n1549\tTABLE III\n1550\tE NVIRONMENT- AWARE GENERATION RESULTS UNDER NOISE - ONLY AND\n1551\tREVERB - ONLY CONDITIONS ON THE SIMULATED TEST SET\n1552\tSystem\n1553\t\n1554\tWER (%)\n1555\t\n1556\tSECS ↑\n1557\t\n1558\tCLAP SIM ↑\n1559\t\n1560\tTABLE V\n1561\tE FFECT OF TCFG SCALES (αspeech :αnoise :αreverb ) ON\n1562\tENVIRONMENT- AWARE GENERATION . ∗ DENOTES THE CONFIGURATION\n1563\tUSED IN THE MAIN EXPERIMENTS .\n1564\t\n1565\tFAD (VGGish) ↓\n1566\t\n1567\tαs :αn :αr\n1568\t\n1569\tNoise-only Prompts\n1570\tGround Truth\n1571\tVocoder\n1572\t\n1573\t3.38\n1574\t3.48\n1575\t\n1576\t0.699\n1577\t0.653\n1578\t\n1579\t0.788\n1580\t0.784\n1581\t\n1582\t0.13\n1583\t0.18\n1584\t\n1585\tF5-TTS\n1586\tDAIEN-TTS-N\n1587\tDAIEN-TTS-NR\n1588\t\n1589\t2.58\n1590\t3.03\n1591\t2.90\n1592\t\n1593\t0.506\n1594\t0.543\n1595\t0.528\n1596\t\n1597\t0.612\n1598\t0.735\n1599\t0.716\n1600\t\n1601\t3.83\n1602\t1.54\n1603\t1.25\n1604\t\n1605\tGround Truth\n1606\tVocoder\n1607\t\n1608\t5.29\n1609\t5.20\n1610\t\n1611\t0.605\n1612\t0.574\n1613\t\n1614\t0.873\n1615\t0.874\n1616\t\n1617\t0.04\n1618\t0.08\n1619\t\n1620\tF5-TTS\n1621\tDAIEN-TTS-N\n1622\tDAIEN-TTS-NR\n1623\t\n1624\t2.68\n1625\t17.56\n1626\t13.53\n1627\t\n1628\t0.391\n1629\t0.317\n1630\t0.400\n1631\t\n1632\t0.814\n1633\t0.770\n1634\t0.794\n1635\t\n1636\t7.11\n1637\t5.06\n1638\t2.86\n1639\t\n1640\tCLAP SIM ↑\n1641\t\n1642\tFAD (VGGish) ↓\n1643\t\n1644\t8.32\n1645\t13.05\n1646\t31.53\n1647\t63.83\n1648\t\n1649\t0.387\n1650\t0.361\n1651\t0.299\n1652\t0.209\n1653\t\n1654\t0.751\n1655\t0.770\n1656\t0.741\n1657\t0.674\n1658\t\n1659\t2.36\n1660\t2.11\n1661\t2.90\n1662\t4.43\n1663\t\n1664\tVarying αreverb (fixed αspeech = 1, αnoise = 3)\n1665\t\n1666\tTABLE IV\n1667\tCLAP SIMILARITY AND FAD BETWEEN THE NOISY- REVERBERANT\n1668\tENVIRONMENT PROMPT AND GROUND - TRUTH RECORDINGS UNDER\n1669\tDIFFERENT ACOUSTIC CONDITIONS\n1670\t\n1671\tClean\n1672\tNoise-Only\n1673\tReverb-Only\n1674\tNoisy-Reverberant\n1675\t\n1676\tSECS ↑\n1677\t\n1678\tVarying αnoise (fixed αspeech = 1, αreverb = 6)\n1679\t1:0:6\n1680\t1:3:6∗\n1681\t1:6:6\n1682\t1:9:6\n1683\t\n1684\tReverb-only Prompts\n1685\t\n1686\tEvaluation Set\n1687\t\n1688\tWER (%)\n1689\t\n1690\tCLAP SIM ↑\n1691\t\n1692\tFAD (VGGish) ↓\n1693\t\n1694\t0.540\n1695\t0.672\n1696\t0.729\n1697\t0.851\n1698\t\n1699\t17.05\n1700\t12.72\n1701\t2.78\n1702\t0.10\n1703\t\n1704\tWe further analyzed the characteristics of the two environmental fidelity metrics by computing both between the noisyreverberant environment prompt and ground-truth recordings\n1705\tunder four conditions, as shown in Table IV. The results reveal\n1706\tthat the two metrics capture different aspects of environmental\n1707\tfidelity: CLAP similarity, based on high-level audio scene embeddings, reflects the overall acoustic similarity, while FAD,\n1708\tcomputed at the distribution level, is particularly effective\n1709\tat capturing reverberation characteristics, as evidenced by its\n1710\tsharp decrease from 17.05 (clean) to 2.78 (reverb-only).\n1711\tThe results from Table IV also help explain the observation in Table III, which shows that F5-TTS achieved a\n1712\trelatively high CLAP similarity (0.814) under the reverb-only\n1713\tcondition. As an ICL-based system, F5-TTS tends to preserve\n1714\tthe reverberation from the speaker prompt, contributing to a\n1715\tmoderate CLAP similarity, but its high FAD (7.11) reveals\n1716\tthat the reverberation characteristics do not faithfully match\n1717\tthose of the environment prompt. The complementary nature\n1718\tof the two metrics justifies our adoption of both for evaluating\n1719\tenvironmental fidelity.\n1720\tThese results, together with the noisy-reverberant results\n1721\tin Table II, indicate that DAIEN-TTS-NR maintains robust\n1722\tperformance across diverse environment conditions, while\n1723\tDAIEN-TTS-N is limited to noise-dominated scenarios.\n1724\t2) Effect of TCFG Scales: To demonstrate the independent\n1725\tcontrollability of the TCFG mechanism, we varied αnoise and\n1726\tαreverb separately while keeping the other scales fixed and\n1727\tevaluated the results on the simulated noisy reverberant test\n1728\tset. Table V presents the results. The guidance scales used in\n1729\tthe main experiments (αspeech = 1, αnoise = 3, αreverb = 6)\n1730\twere empirically selected to balance speech intelligibility and\n1731\tenvironmental fidelity, and different operating points may be\n1732\t\n1733\t1:3:0\n1734\t1:3:3\n1735\t1:3:6∗\n1736\t1:3:9\n1737\t\n1738\t5.87\n1739\t7.35\n1740\t13.05\n1741\t25.64\n1742\t\n1743\t0.403\n1744\t0.386\n1745\t0.361\n1746\t0.334\n1747\t\n1748\t0.722\n1749\t0.758\n1750\t0.770\n1751\t0.769\n1752\t\n1753\t10.38\n1754\t4.18\n1755\t2.11\n1756\t1.48\n1757\t\n1758\tchosen depending on the target application.\n1759\tWhen increasing αnoise with fixed αreverb = 6, CLAP\n1760\tsimilarity first improved from 0.751 (αnoise = 0) to 0.770\n1761\t(αnoise = 3) then decreased at higher values, while FAD\n1762\tremains relatively stable within the moderate range (2.36 to\n1763\t2.90), indicating that the noise guidance primarily affects\n1764\tnoise-related characteristics without substantially altering the\n1765\treverberation reconstruction. However, excessive noise guidance leads to severe degradation in intelligibility, with WER\n1766\trising sharply from 13.05 (αnoise = 3) to 31.53 (αnoise = 6)\n1767\tand 63.83 (αnoise = 9).\n1768\tWhen increasing αreverb with fixed αnoise = 3, FAD\n1769\tdecreased dramatically from 10.38 (αreverb = 0) to 1.48\n1770\t(αreverb = 9), while CLAP similarity remains relatively\n1771\tstable after αreverb = 3 (0.758 to 0.770), confirming that the\n1772\treverberation guidance primarily affects reverberation-related\n1773\tcharacteristics as captured by FAD, with limited impact on\n1774\tthe noise component. This observation is consistent with the\n1775\tmetric sensitivity analysis in Table IV, where FAD was shown\n1776\tto be more sensitive to reverberation than CLAP similarity.\n1777\tThese results verify that the TCFG mechanism enables\n1778\teffective and independent control over the noise and reverberation components of the synthesized environmental speech.\n1779\tVI. C ONCLUSIONS AND F UTURE W ORK\n1780\tThis paper presented an extended DAIEN-TTS framework\n1781\tfor environment-aware zero-shot TTS towards real-world scenarios. The framework jointly models speech, noise, and reverberation through an SES module and cross-attention-based\n1782\tenvironment conditioning mechanism, enabling independent\n1783\tcontrol over the timbre and acoustic environment via separate\n1784\taudio prompts. A two-stage training pipeline, pretraining on\n1785\tsimulated data and fine-tuning on real-world data, was adopted\n1786\tto bridge the simulated-to-real domain gap.\n1787\tThe experimental results indicate that joint modeling of\n1788\tnoise and reverberation proved critical for both environmentrobust and environment-aware synthesis, with DAIEN-TTS\n1789\tachieving a MOS comparable to the ground truth under\n1790\tsimulated prompts and the highest environmental fidelity with\n1791\tan ESMOS approaching the ground-truth reference. Finetuning on real-world data effectively improved both speaker\n1792\t\n1793\t\fJOURNAL OF LATEX CLASS FILES, VOL. 14, NO. 8, AUGUST 2015\n1794\t\n1795\tsimilarity and environmental fidelity. The TCFG mechanism\n1796\tenabled largely independent control over the noise and reverberation components, and our analysis further revealed that\n1797\tCLAP similarity and FAD capture complementary aspects of\n1798\tenvironmental fidelity.\n1799\tFor future work, we plan to explore alternative environment\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "7ac83494-635c-4c75-8a11-1d3a2be4f75e", "uuid": "9b4c07ec-3db1-45bd-8e33-6487cdb2e9d8", "timestamp": "2026-08-05T11:36:12.140Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/daien-tts.txt", "content": "(SSMOS) test was conducted by providing the speaker prompt\nas a reference and asking listeners to rate how closely the synthesized speech matches the target speaker’s voice, focusing on\nspeaker identity while ignoring the acoustic environment. An\nenvironment similarity MOS (ESMOS) test was conducted by\nproviding the environment prompt as a reference and asking\nlisteners to rate the consistency in terms of background noise,\nnon-speech sound events, and reverberation, while ignoring\nspeaker identity and speech content. All listening tests were\ncrowd-sourced via Amazon Mechanical Turk with more than\n30 native English-speaking raters, each rating 20 samples per\ntest. We report the mean scores with 95% confidence intervals\n(CI).\nV. R ESULTS AND A NALYSIS\nA. Environment-robust Generation\nWe first evaluated the ability of each system to synthesize\nclean personalized speech when given a silence environment\nprompt, under two speaker prompt conditions: clean and simulated noisy-reverberant. In this scenario, the system is expected\nto disentangle the environmental factors from the speaker\nprompt and produce clean, natural speech. For the MOS test\nin this scenario, listeners were asked to evaluate both the\nnaturalness and acoustic cleanliness of the synthesized speech,\nwith any residual noise or reverberation artifacts considered as\nquality degradation. For the SSMOS test, the clean version of\nthe speaker prompt was provided as the reference since paired\nclean-environmental speech is available in the simulated test\nset. Table I presents the evaluation results, where the groundtruth recordings and their vocoder-resynthesized versions serve\nas upper bounds.\nUnder the clean speaker prompt condition, all TTS systems achieved comparable performance across all metrics.\nThe DAIEN-TTS variants outperformed F5-TTS in WER and\nSECS, which can be attributed to the data augmentation effect\nintroduced by training on paired clean-environmental speech\ndata. The MOS and SSMOS scores of all systems were\nclose, with overlapping confidence intervals, indicating that\nthe additional environment modeling in DAIEN-TTS does not\ndegrade synthesis quality when clean prompts are provided.\nUnder the simulated noisy-reverberant speaker prompt condition, clear performance differences emerged among the\nsystems. F5-TTS, which is trained exclusively on clean speech,\nexhibited substantial degradation in both intelligibility and\nspeaker similarity compared with the clean prompt condition, confirming the sensitivity of ICL-based TTS to adverse\n\n8\n\nTABLE I\nO BJECTIVE AND SUBJECTIVE EVALUATION RESULTS OF\nENVIRONMENT- ROBUST GENERATION ON THE S EED -TTS TEST- EN SET\nSystem\n\nWER (%) ↓\n\nSECS ↑\n\nMOS (CI) ↑\n\nSSMOS (CI) ↑\n\nClean Speaker Prompt\nGround Truth\nVocoder\n\n2.14\n2.18\n\n0.734\n0.698\n\n3.91 (± 0.08)\n–\n\n3.52 (± 0.09)\n–\n\nF5-TTS\nDAIEN-TTS-N\nDAIEN-TTS-NR\n\n2.30\n2.07\n2.03\n\n0.590\n0.617\n0.628\n\n3.86 (± 0.08)\n3.91 (± 0.08)\n3.90 (± 0.08)\n\n3.46 (± 0.09)\n3.49 (± 0.08)\n3.48 (± 0.08)\n\nSimulated Noisy-reverberant Speaker Prompt\nGround Truth\nVocoder\nF5-TTS\nDAIEN-TTS-N\nDAIEN-TTS-NR\n\n2.14\n2.18\n2.80\n2.75\n2.24\n\n0.734\n0.698\n0.317\n0.380\n0.486\n\n3.95 (± 0.07)\n–\n3.75 (± 0.08)\n3.77 (± 0.07)\n3.91 (± 0.08)\n\n3.43 (± 0.08)\n–\n3.19 (± 0.09)\n3.30 (± 0.08)\n3.37 (± 0.08)\n\nacoustic conditions in the speaker prompt. DAIEN-TTS-N\nshowed a similar trend, as its noise-only separation module\ncannot adequately remove the reverberation component from\nthe speaker prompt. In contrast, DAIEN-TTS-NR achieved\nthe best performance across all metrics, with its MOS score\ncomparable to the ground-truth reference, demonstrating that\njoint modeling of noise and reverberation is essential for robust\nsynthesis under adverse acoustic conditions.\n\nB. Environment-aware Generation\nWe next evaluated the ability of each system to synthesize environmental personalized speech that reproduces the\nacoustic environment specified by the environment prompt.\nExperiments were conducted under two conditions: simulated\nspeaker and environment prompts and real-world prompts.\nIn contrast to the environment-robust scenario, listeners in\nthe MOS test were instructed to evaluate the overall naturalness of the environmental speech, including both the speech\nand acoustic environment, without penalizing the presence\nof background noise and reverberation. For the SSMOS test\nunder the simulated condition, the clean version of the speaker\nprompt was provided as the reference since paired cleanenvironmental speech is available. Under the real-world condition, the original speaker prompt was used as the reference,\nas paired clean recordings are unavailable. Table II presents\nthe evaluation results.\n1) Simulated Prompts: Under the simulated prompt condition, the ground-truth recordings and their vocoderresynthesized versions serve as upper bounds. Note that WER\nin this scenario should be interpreted with caution. The\nground-truth environmental speech already exhibits a WER\nof 15.41%, significantly higher than the 2.14% observed for\nclean speech in Table I, indicating that the ASR system is\nsubstantially affected by the presence of noise and reverberation. Similarly, higher WERs for the environment-aware\nsystems may reflect the expected presence of background noise\nand reverberation in the synthesized speech, rather than a\ndegradation in synthesis quality. F5-TTS achieved the lowest\nWER as it lacks an environment conditioning mechanism and\ndoes not actively reproduce the target acoustic environment.\n\n\fJOURNAL OF LATEX CLASS FILES, VOL. 14, NO. 8, AUGUST 2015\n\n9\n\nTABLE II\nO BJECTIVE AND SUBJECTIVE EVALUATION RESULTS OF ENVIRONMENT- AWARE GENERATION ON THE S EED -TTS TEST- EN SET ( SIMULATED PROMPTS )\nAND TITW TEST SET ( REAL - WORLD PROMPTS )\nSystem\n\nWER (%)\n\nSECS ↑\n\nCLAP SIM ↑\n\nFAD (VGGish) ↓\n\nMOS (CI) ↑\n\nSSMOS (CI) ↑\n\nESMOS (CI) ↑\n\nSimulated Noisy-reverberant Prompts\nGround Truth\nVocoder\n\n15.41\n15.80\n\n0.530\n0.493\n\n0.851\n0.848\n\n0.10\n0.13\n\n3.40 (± 0.08)\n–\n\n3.58 (± 0.09)\n–\n\n3.59 (± 0.07)\n–\n\nF5-TTS\nDAIEN-TTS-N\nDAIEN-TTS-NR\n\n2.80\n10.85\n13.05\n\n0.317\n0.325\n0.361\n\n0.685\n0.743\n0.770\n\n8.56\n5.73\n2.11\n\n3.19 (± 0.08)\n3.27 (± 0.08)\n3.38 (± 0.08)\n\n3.41 (± 0.09)\n3.48 (± 0.09)\n3.53 (± 0.08)\n\n3.27 (± 0.09)\n3.45 (± 0.08)\n3.55 (± 0.08)\n\nF5-TTS\nDAIEN-TTS-N\nDAIEN-TTS-NR\nDAIEN-TTS-FT\n\n12.42\n16.07\n10.05\n10.65\n\n0.481\n0.447\n0.457\n0.486\n\n0.588\n0.618\n0.612\n0.634\n\n3.82 (± 0.08)\n3.71 (± 0.09)\n3.74 (± 0.08)\n3.84 (± 0.09)\n\n3.46 (± 0.09)\n3.55 (± 0.08)\n3.58 (± 0.08)\n3.62 (± 0.08)\n\nReal-world Environmental Prompts\n\nAmong the environment-aware systems, DAIEN-TTS-N\npartially reproduced the background noise but lacked reverberation modeling, resulting in moderate environmental\nfidelity (CLAP: 0.743, FAD: 5.73). DAIEN-TTS-NR achieved\nthe best environmental fidelity (CLAP: 0.770, FAD: 2.11),\nsubstantially narrowing the gap to the vocoder upper bound\n(FAD: 0.13), while also attaining the highest speaker similarity\n(SECS: 0.361) among all TTS systems.\nThe subjective evaluation results further corroborate these\nfindings. DAIEN-TTS-NR achieved the highest scores across\nall three perceptual dimensions (MOS: 3.38, SSMOS: 3.53,\nESMOS: 3.55), with its ESMOS approaching the groundtruth reference (3.59), indicating that the reconstructed acoustic environment is perceptually close to the target. F5-TTS\nreceived the lowest MOS (3.19) and ESMOS (3.27), as it is\ntrained on clean speech and struggles to faithfully reconstruct\nthe heavy noise and reverberation present in the speaker\nprompt, resulting in degraded environmental quality despite\npartially preserving some background characteristics. These\nresults confirm that joint modeling of noise and reverberation\nsignificantly improves the fidelity of environment reconstruction in both objective and subjective evaluations.\n2) Real-world Prompts: Under the real-world prompt condition, we further included the fine-tuned variant DAIEN-TTSFT to evaluate the effectiveness of real-world data adaptation.\nNote that the WERs under the real-world condition were\ngenerally higher than those under the simulated condition for\nall systems, partly because the transcriptions of the real-world\nspeaker prompts were obtained via ASR and may contain\nerrors, which propagate through the text input and affect the\nsynthesized speech.\nAs expected, F5-TTS yielded the lowest CLAP similarity\n(0.588) and highest FAD (8.37), as it lacks the ability to reproduce acoustic environments by design. However, it achieved\na relatively high MOS (3.40) and the highest SSMOS (3.82)\nunder this condition, as the speaker prompts were selected to\nbe relatively clean (DNSMOS BAK > 3.5), resulting in higher\nspeech quality despite poor environment reconstruction.\nDAIEN-TTS-N achieved higher environmental fidelity\n(CLAP: 0.618, FAD: 4.92), but its noise-only separation\nmodule struggled with the complex acoustic conditions in\n\n8.37\n4.92\n5.33\n3.62\n\n3.40 (± 0.08)\n3.29 (± 0.08)\n3.34 (± 0.08)\n3.38 (± 0.08)\n\nreal-world recordings, leading to noticeable degradation in\nspeech intelligibility. The additional reverberation modeling in\nDAIEN-TTS-NR did not yield a clear advantage over DAIENTTS-N in environmental fidelity under this condition. An\nanalysis of the TITW test samples revealed that the acoustic\nenvironments are predominantly characterized by background\nnoise, with reverberation playing a less prominent role, which\nlimits the benefit of joint noise-reverberation modeling.\nAfter fine-tuning on real-world data, DAIEN-TTS-FT\nachieved improvements across all metrics, attaining the\nbest speaker similarity (SECS: 0.486), environmental fidelity\n(CLAP: 0.634, FAD: 3.62), and subjective scores (MOS: 3.38,\nSSMOS: 3.84, ESMOS: 3.62), demonstrating the effectiveness\nof the two-stage training strategy in bridging the simulatedto-real domain gap.\nC. Analysis Experiment\n1) Effect of Environment Conditions: To further investigate\nthe behavior of each system under different types of acoustic environments, we evaluated environment-aware generation\nwith noise-only and reverb-only environment prompts on the\nsimulated test set. Table III presents the results, complementing the noisy-reverberant results in Table II.\nUnder the noise-only condition, F5-TTS yielded the lowest\nCLAP similarity and highest FAD, consistent with its inability\nto reproduce acoustic environments. Both DAIEN-TTS-N and\nDAIEN-TTS-NR achieved substantially better environmental\nfidelity, with DAIEN-TTS-N attaining slightly higher CLAP\nsimilarity as it is specifically designed for noise-only modeling. The marginal difference between the two systems indicates that the additional reverberation modeling in DAIENTTS-NR does not significantly interfere with noise disentanglement and reconstruction in noise-dominated scenarios.\nUnder the reverb-only condition, a substantial performance\ngap emerged. DAIEN-TTS-N, which lacks reverberation modeling, exhibited poor environmental fidelity (FAD: 5.06), as\nits noise-only separation module fails to properly handle\nreverberant speech. In contrast, DAIEN-TTS-NR achieved\nnotably better environmental fidelity (FAD: 2.86) and speaker\nsimilarity (SECS: 0.400 vs. 0.317), confirming the necessity\nof explicit reverberation modeling.\n\n\fJOURNAL OF LATEX CLASS FILES, VOL. 14, NO. 8, AUGUST 2015\n\n10\n\nTABLE III\nE NVIRONMENT- AWARE GENERATION RESULTS UNDER NOISE - ONLY AND\nREVERB - ONLY CONDITIONS ON THE SIMULATED TEST SET\nSystem\n\nWER (%)\n\nSECS ↑\n\nCLAP SIM ↑\n\nTABLE V\nE FFECT OF TCFG SCALES (αspeech :αnoise :αreverb ) ON\nENVIRONMENT- AWARE GENERATION . ∗ DENOTES THE CONFIGURATION\nUSED IN THE MAIN EXPERIMENTS .\n\nFAD (VGGish) ↓\n\nαs :αn :αr\n\nNoise-only Prompts\nGround Truth\nVocoder\n\n3.38\n3.48\n\n0.699\n0.653\n\n0.788\n0.784\n\n0.13\n0.18\n\nF5-TTS\nDAIEN-TTS-N\nDAIEN-TTS-NR\n\n2.58\n3.03\n2.90\n\n0.506\n0.543\n0.528\n\n0.612\n0.735\n0.716\n\n3.83\n1.54\n1.25\n\nGround Truth\nVocoder\n\n5.29\n5.20\n\n0.605\n0.574\n\n0.873\n0.874\n\n0.04\n0.08\n\nF5-TTS\nDAIEN-TTS-N\nDAIEN-TTS-NR\n\n2.68\n17.56\n13.53\n\n0.391\n0.317\n0.400\n\n0.814\n0.770\n0.794\n\n7.11\n5.06\n2.86\n\nCLAP SIM ↑\n\nFAD (VGGish) ↓\n\n8.32\n13.05\n31.53\n63.83\n\n0.387\n0.361\n0.299\n0.209\n\n0.751\n0.770\n0.741\n0.674\n\n2.36\n2.11\n2.90\n4.43\n\nVarying αreverb (fixed αspeech = 1, αnoise = 3)\n\nTABLE IV\nCLAP SIMILARITY AND FAD BETWEEN THE NOISY- REVERBERANT\nENVIRONMENT PROMPT AND GROUND - TRUTH RECORDINGS UNDER\nDIFFERENT ACOUSTIC CONDITIONS\n\nClean\nNoise-Only\nReverb-Only\nNoisy-Reverberant\n\nSECS ↑\n\nVarying αnoise (fixed αspeech = 1, αreverb = 6)\n1:0:6\n1:3:6∗\n1:6:6\n1:9:6\n\nReverb-only Prompts\n\nEvaluation Set\n\nWER (%)\n\nCLAP SIM ↑\n\nFAD (VGGish) ↓\n\n0.540\n0.672\n0.729\n0.851\n\n17.05\n12.72\n2.78\n0.10\n\nWe further analyzed the characteristics of the two environmental fidelity metrics by computing both between the noisyreverberant environment prompt and ground-truth recordings\nunder four conditions, as shown in Table IV. The results reveal\nthat the two metrics capture different aspects of environmental\nfidelity: CLAP similarity, based on high-level audio scene embeddings, reflects the overall acoustic similarity, while FAD,\ncomputed at the distribution level, is particularly effective\nat capturing reverberation characteristics, as evidenced by its\nsharp decrease from 17.05 (clean) to 2.78 (reverb-only).\nThe results from Table IV also help explain the observation in Table III, which shows that F5-TTS achieved a\nrelatively high CLAP similarity (0.814) under the reverb-only\ncondition. As an ICL-based system, F5-TTS tends to preserve\nthe reverberation from the speaker prompt, contributing to a\nmoderate CLAP similarity, but its high FAD (7.11) reveals\nthat the reverberation characteristics do not faithfully match\nthose of the environment prompt. The complementary nature\nof the two metrics justifies our adoption of both for evaluating\nenvironmental fidelity.\nThese results, together with the noisy-reverberant results\nin Table II, indicate that DAIEN-TTS-NR maintains robust\nperformance across diverse environment conditions, while\nDAIEN-TTS-N is limited to noise-dominated scenarios.\n2) Effect of TCFG Scales: To demonstrate the independent\ncontrollability of the TCFG mechanism, we varied αnoise and\nαreverb separately while keeping the other scales fixed and\nevaluated the results on the simulated noisy reverberant test\nset. Table V presents the results. The guidance scales used in\nthe main experiments (αspeech = 1, αnoise = 3, αreverb = 6)\nwere empirically selected to balance speech intelligibility and\nenvironmental fidelity, and different operating points may be\n\n1:3:0\n1:3:3\n1:3:6∗\n1:3:9\n\n5.87\n7.35\n13.05\n25.64\n\n0.403\n0.386\n0.361\n0.334\n\n0.722\n0.758\n0.770\n0.769\n\n10.38\n4.18\n2.11\n1.48\n\nchosen depending on the target application.\nWhen increasing αnoise with fixed αreverb = 6, CLAP\nsimilarity first improved from 0.751 (αnoise = 0) to 0.770\n(αnoise = 3) then decreased at higher values, while FAD\nremains relatively stable within the moderate range (2.36 to\n2.90), indicating that the noise guidance primarily affects\nnoise-related characteristics without substantially altering the\nreverberation reconstruction. However, excessive noise guidance leads to severe degradation in intelligibility, with WER\nrising sharply from 13.05 (αnoise = 3) to 31.53 (αnoise = 6)\nand 63.83 (αnoise = 9).\nWhen increasing αreverb with fixed αnoise = 3, FAD\ndecreased dramatically from 10.38 (αreverb = 0) to 1.48\n(αreverb = 9), while CLAP similarity remains relatively\nstable after αreverb = 3 (0.758 to 0.770), confirming that the\nreverberation guidance primarily affects reverberation-related\ncharacteristics as captured by FAD, with limited impact on\nthe noise component. This observation is consistent with the\nmetric sensitivity analysis in Table IV, where FAD was shown\nto be more sensitive to reverberation than CLAP similarity.\nThese results verify that the TCFG mechanism enables\neffective and independent control over the noise and reverberation components of the synthesized environmental speech.\nVI. C ONCLUSIONS AND F UTURE W ORK\nThis paper presented an extended DAIEN-TTS framework\nfor environment-aware zero-shot TTS towards real-world scenarios. The framework jointly models speech, noise, and reverberation through an SES module and cross-attention-based\nenvironment conditioning mechanism, enabling independent\ncontrol over the timbre and acoustic environment via separate\naudio prompts. A two-stage training pipeline, pretraining on\nsimulated data and fine-tuning on real-world data, was adopted\nto bridge the simulated-to-real domain gap.\nThe experimental results indicate that joint modeling of\nnoise and reverberation proved critical for both environmentrobust and environment-aware synthesis, with DAIEN-TTS\nachieving a MOS comparable to the ground truth under\nsimulated prompts and the highest environmental fidelity with\nan ESMOS approaching the ground-truth reference. Finetuning on real-world data effectively improved both speaker\n\n\fJOURNAL OF LATEX CLASS FILES, VOL. 14, NO. 8, AUGUST 2015\n\nsimilarity and environmental fidelity. The TCFG mechanism\nenabled largely independent control over the noise and reverberation components, and our analysis further revealed that\nCLAP similarity and FAD capture complementary aspects of\nenvironmental fidelity.\nFor future work, we plan to explore alternative environment", "numLines": 600, "startLine": 1200, "totalLines": 1961}}}
